e] [17592186133961 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241111 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186133963 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241124 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186134066 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130522 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186134066 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240674 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186134066 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186134066 1071 "Let $V = R_{1}[t]$, the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n$⟨p(t), q(t)⟩ = p(0)q(0) + p(1)q(1)$,\nfind the orthogonal projection of $p(t) =-3t - 2$ onto the subspace spanned by the polynomial $q(t) = t$." 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186134066 1071 "Let $V={R}_{1}[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $p(t)=-3t-2$ onto the subspace spanned by the polynomial $q(t)=t.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186134066 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{q(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)=\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(-3t-2){|}_{0}\\cdot (t){|}_{0}+(-3t-2){|}_{1}\\cdot (t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 0+(-5)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0-5 \\\\  & =-5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),q(t)⟩ & =q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(t){|}_{0}\\cdot (t){|}_{0}+(t){|}_{1}\\cdot (t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{1}\\cdot t \\\\  & =-5t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186134068 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130534 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186134068 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240745 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186134068 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186134068 1071 "![](images/38/38a7ae83a66a2c1ff6a012b9405c6621b0c0a162018065549e75a1d08bc04d44.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x, y, z)$, shown above. Given that $F(x, y, z)$ is independent of $z$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $curlF = 0$\n2. $curlF \\ne 0$\n3. $F$ is irrotational\n4. $curlF$ points in the negative $z$-direction." 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186134068 1071 "![](images/38/38a7ae83a66a2c1ff6a012b9405c6621b0c0a162018065549e75a1d08bc04d44.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $F$ is irrotational\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186134068 1074 "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](images/59/594106633e422d24b9fea113d24fb6367fdef5ac52724931860e4163e33ce992.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statements III is true. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186134070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130546 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186134070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240481 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186134070 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186134070 1071 "Let $V = C[- π, π]$, the vector space of continuous functions over $[- π, π]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n$⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ = \\displaystyle \\int_{- π}^{π}x(t)y(t)dt$.\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V$:\n\n$f_{1}(t) = \\sin t, f_{2}(t) = \\cos t, g(t) = \\sin^{2} t - \\cos t$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S = Span{f_{1}(t), f_{2}(t)}$ given that $f_{1}(t)⊥f_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $Span{f_{2}(t)}$ equals $-\\cos t$." 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186134070 1071 "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,g(t)={sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}$ equals $-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186134070 1074 "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{1}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(t){f}_{1}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is an odd function, while ${sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is even. Hence, their product $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{0}{⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186147459 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241158 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186147459 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241159 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186147729 1068 #edb/ref 17592186144107 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186147729 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240718 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186147729 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186147729 1071 "The substitution $z = y^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + x^{2}y = xy^{4}$\ninto an equation of the form $z^{′}(x) + P(x)z(x) = Q(x)$. What is the function $P(x)$?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186147729 1071 "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y=x{y}^{4}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186147729 1074 "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{2},\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=4.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{4},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-4}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}{y}^{-3}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-3{y}^{-4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-4}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}z=x\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-3{x}^{2}z=-3x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-3{x}^{2}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186147730 1068 #edb/ref 17592186144113 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186147730 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240729 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186147730 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186147730 1071 "The substitution $z = y^{m}$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{y}{x} = x\\sqrt{y}$\ninto an equation of the form $z^{′}(x) + P(x)z(x) = Q(x)$. What is the function $Q(x)$?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186147730 1071 "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{y}{x}=x\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186147730 1074 "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{{y}^{1/2}}{x}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{1/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}\\,z=x\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{2x}\\,z=\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x)=\\frac{x}{2}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186147731 1068 #edb/ref 17592186144119 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186147731 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240374 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186147731 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186147731 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y={y}^{2}{e}^{x}.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186147731 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} - y = y^{2}e^{x}$." 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186147731 1074 "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-1,\\,q(x)={e}^{x},\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-{y}^{-1}={e}^{x}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-z={e}^{x}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z=-{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=1,\\,Q(x)=-{e}^{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot (-{e}^{x}) \\\\ {e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z{e}^{x} & =-{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{x}) & =-{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{x} & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-{e}^{2x}}{2{e}^{x}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-{e}^{2x}}{2{e}^{x}}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{C-{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186152193 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241167 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186152193 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241168 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186152193 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241169 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186152194 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241176 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186152194 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241177 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186152194 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241178 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186152194 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241179 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186152195 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241170 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186152195 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241171 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186152196 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241172 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186152196 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241173 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186152196 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241174 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186152196 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241175 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186157290 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241018 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186157290 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241019 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186157290 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241021 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186157290 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241135 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186157290 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241136 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186157291 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241022 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186157291 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241024 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186157291 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241026 1319413953339