92186240907 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186166870 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240876 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186166870 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240878 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186167057 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163390 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186167057 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240659 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186167057 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186167057 1071 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $R^{2}$?\n\n1. $F = xe^{xy}i + ye^{xy}j$\n2. $G = (xy + y^{2})i + (xy + x^{2})j$\n3. $H = \\cos (x - y)i - \\cos (x - y)j$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186167057 1071 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F=x{e}^{xy}\\,i+y{e}^{xy}\\,j$\n- $G=(xy+{y}^{2})\\,i+(xy+{x}^{2})\\,j$\n- $H=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,j$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186167057 1074 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=x{e}^{xy}$ and $Q=y{e}^{xy}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={x}^{2}{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=xy+{y}^{2}$ and $Q=xy+{x}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x+2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y+2x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)$ and $Q=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y).\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172037 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240937 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172037 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240938 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172037 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240939 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172038 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240950 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172038 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240952 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172039 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240969 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172039 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240970 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172040 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240971 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172040 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240972 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172040 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240974 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172063 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241109 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172063 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241110 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172063 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241119 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172063 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241120 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172063 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241121 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172064 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241093 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172064 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241096 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172064 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241098 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172064 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241100 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172064 1038 #edb/ref 1759218