 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,-1,\\,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172230 1068 #edb/ref 17592186168213 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186172230 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240683 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172230 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172230 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface generated by the vector-valued function $r(u, v) = ⟨2u, u\\sin v, 4u^{2}⟩$ at the point where $u = 1$ and $v = π$." 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186172230 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface generated by the vector-valued function $r(u,v)=⟨2u,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,4{u}^{2}⟩$ at the point where $u=1$ and $v=\\pi .$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172230 1074 "We first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(4{u}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,8u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(4{u}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these vectors when $u=1$ and $v=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(1,\\pi ) & =⟨2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ),\\,8(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,0,\\,8⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(1,\\pi ) & =⟨0,\\,(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(1,\\pi )\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(1,\\pi ) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,\\,0,\\,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the parameters $u=1$ and $v=\\pi $ correspond to the point \n\n$$\n(2(1),\\,(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ),\\,4(1)^{2})=(2,0,4),\n$$\n\nthe equation of the tangent plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨8,\\,0,\\,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y,\\,z-4⟩ & =0 \\\\ 8\\cdot (x-2)+0\\cdot y+(-2)\\cdot (z-4) & =0 \\\\ 8x-2z-8 & =0 \\\\ 4x-z & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186168237 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186172234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240589 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172234 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172234 1071 "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨\\text{_____},\\,4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r⟩,\\,r\\ge 0\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${z}^{2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}$ for $z\\ge 0.$ What is the first component of $f?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186172234 1071 "$f(r, θ) = ⟨_____, 4r\\sin θ, r⟩, r \\ge 0$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r, θ)$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z^{2} = \\frac{x^{2}}{16} + \\frac{y^{2}}{16}$ for $z \\ge 0$. What is the first component of $f$?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186172234 1074 "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx=4X,\\,y=4Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our half-cone, we get\n\n$$\n{Z}^{2}={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2},\\,Z\\ge 0\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a half-cone whose axis of symmetry is the $Z$-axis in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates. In cylindrical polar coordinates, this cone has the equation $Z=r.$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4X,\\,4Y\\,,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186175938 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240896 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186175954 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241160 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186175969 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240897 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186175969 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240898 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186175970 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240834 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186175970 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240835 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186175970 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240836 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186175970 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240837 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186175971 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240829 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186175971 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240830 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186175971 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240831 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186175971 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240832 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186175971 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240833 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186175982 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240838 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186175982 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240839 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186175982 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240840 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186175982 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240841 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186175982 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240855 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186175982 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240857 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173440 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186176202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240408 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176202 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176202 1071 "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=16.$ By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the second component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{z}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r,z⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176202 1071 "The vector-valued function $f(θ, ϕ)$, given by\n\n$f(θ, ϕ) = ⟨4\\cosh ϕ\\cos θ, _____, 4\\sinh ϕ⟩$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x^{2} + y^{2} - z^{2} = 16$. By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the second component of $f$.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola $r^{2} - z^{2} = k^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r, z⟩ = ⟨k\\cosh ϕ, k\\sinh ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ ∈ R$." 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186176202 1074 "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{z}^{2}=16$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $x$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨r,z⟩=⟨4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173446 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186176203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240577 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176203 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176203 1071 "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-{y}^{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3X,\\,y=Z,\\,z=2Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{Z}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r,Z⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176203 1071 "The vector-valued function $f(θ, ϕ)$, given by\n\n$f(θ, ϕ) = ⟨_____, \\sinh ϕ, 2\\cosh ϕ\\sin θ⟩$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$\\frac{x^{2}}{9} - y^{2} + \\frac{z^{2}}{4} = 1$.\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n$x = 3X, y = Z, z = 2Y$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $f$.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola $r^{2} - Z^{2} = k^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r, Z⟩ = ⟨k\\cosh ϕ, k\\sinh ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ ∈ R$." 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186176203 1074 "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3X,\\,y=Z,\\,z=2Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨r,Z⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3X,Z,2Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,2(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173464 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186176206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240494 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176206 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176206 1071 "What surface is defined by the equation $z = \\frac{π}{4}$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186176206 1071 "What surface is defined by the equation $z=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176206 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that $z$-coordinate of any point on our surface equals $\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ while $r$ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/99/99e84f48fa5fa03782c4e18d94c22c31bd39b902e2a289f5958afb7a963f7945.png)" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173476 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186176208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240553 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176208 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176208 1071 "$$\nf(y,z)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,y,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(y,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $5x=10{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}.$ What is the first component of $f?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176208 1071 "$f(y, z) = ⟨_____, y, z⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(y, z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $5x = 10y^{2} + 2z^{2}$. What is the first component of $f$?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186176208 1074 "Solving our equation for $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n5x=10{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=2{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}{z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}{z}^{2},\\,y,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $2{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}{z}^{2}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173500 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186176212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240524 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176212 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176212 1071 "Given that $x =-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a solution to the equation $36x^{3} + 9x^{2} + 4x + 1 = 0$, find the remaining solutions." 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186176212 1071 "Given that $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a solution to the equation $36{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+4x+1=0,$ find the remaining solutions." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176212 1074 "Let $f(x)=36{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+4x+1.$ Since $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x+\\frac{1}{4})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+\\frac{1}{4})$ using synthetic division.\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $36$ | $9$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-9$ | $0$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $36$ | $0$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nThus, $f(x)=(x+\\frac{1}{4})(36{x}^{2}+4).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $36{x}^{2}+4=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36{x}^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ 36{x}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{3}\\text{i})}^{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=-\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176215 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173518 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186176215 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240752 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176215 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176215 1071 "Consider the arc of the curve $x = \\sqrt{4 - y^{2}}$ that lies between the lines $y = 0$ and $y = \\sqrt{2}$. Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2π$ radians about the $y$-axis." 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186176215 1071 "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}$ that lies between the lines $y=0$ and $y=\\sqrt{2}.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176215 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-2y}{2\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}2\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\pi \\,y{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\sqrt{2}-0) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176220 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173548 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186176220 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240586 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176220 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176220 1071 "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $y = \\sin x + z$ whose projection onto the $xz$-plane is bounded by $z = \\sin (2x)$ and the $x$-axis for $0 \\le x \\le \\frac{π}{2}$." 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186176220 1071 "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z$ whose projection onto the $xz$-plane is bounded by $z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and the $x$-axis for $0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186176220 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z,\\,(x,z)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/be/be5dc3bf3ed4b6b7bee06d583b69e40a3922edede1e61a6f0b68d2ce0767f92a.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂y}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot z\\,{|}_{z=0}^{z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=2+{cos}^{2}⁡x,$ we get $\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ u & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{3}^{2}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({3}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(3\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186179694 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241099 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186179694 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241102 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186179694 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241103 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186179694 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241104 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186179695 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241131 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186179695 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241132 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186179696 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241105 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186179696 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241106 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186179697 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241142 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186179697 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241154 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186179915 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177129 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186179915 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240746 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186179915 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186179915 1071 "Calculate $∇ \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}F)$ for the vector field $F$ where\n$F = xe^{2x}i - ye^{2x}j + xz^{2}k$." 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186179915 1071 "Calculate $∇\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}F)$ for the vector field $F$ where\n\n$$\nF=x{e}^{2x}\\,i-y{e}^{2x}\\,j+x{z}^{2}\\,k.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186179915 1074 "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following  linearity properties for divergence:\n\n- $∇\\cdot (kF)=k∇\\cdot F$\n\n- $∇\\cdot (F+G)=∇\\cdot F+∇\\cdot G.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}F)=\\frac{1}{2}∇\\cdot F.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\cdot F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{2x})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y{e}^{2x})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{z}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}+2xz \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2x}+2xz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}F) & =\\frac{1}{2}∇\\cdot F \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (2x{e}^{2x}+2xz) \\\\  & =x{e}^{2x}+xz.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186179916 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177135 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186179916 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240386 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186179916 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186179916 1071 "Given that $F(x, y, z) = ⟨e^{3x},-2ye^{3x}⟩$ and $f(x, y) = 2x$, calculate $∇ \\cdot (fF)$." 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186179916 1071 "Given that $F(x,y,z)=⟨{e}^{3x},-2y{e}^{3x}⟩$ and $f(x,y)=2x,$ calculate $∇\\cdot (f\\,F).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186179916 1074 "We will use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot (f\\,F)=f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇\\cdot F$ and $∇f.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2,0⟩, \\\\ ∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{3x})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y{e}^{3x})=3{e}^{3x}-2{e}^{3x}={e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (f\\,F) & =f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f \\\\  & =2x({e}^{3x})+⟨{e}^{3x},-2y{e}^{3x}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,0⟩ \\\\  & =2x{e}^{3x}+2{e}^{3x} \\\\  & =2(x+1){e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186184230 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240869 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186192770 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241008 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186192770 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241015 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186192771 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241058 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186192771 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241060 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186192771 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241064 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186192793 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241003 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186192950 1068 #edb/ref 17592186189556 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186192950 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240643 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186192950 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186192950 1071 "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,1\\le y\\le 4}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<5}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le x+1,\\,-1\\le x\\le 1}$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186192950 1071 "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\n1. ${(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: 1 \\le x \\le 4, 1 \\le y \\le 4}$\n2. ${(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: x^{2} + y^{2} < 5}$\n3. ${(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: 0 \\le y \\le x + 1,-1 \\le x \\le 1}$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 false] [17592186192950 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186238086 1081 "2{y}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186238086 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186238086 1084 #uuid "00280b32-71c4-532d-9ed6-1f60c4e33219" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186238087 1081 "10" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186238087 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186238087 1084 #uuid "00535c78-693d-578f-9854-35ede0f5ea84" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186238088 1081 "II only" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186238088 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186238088 1084 #uuid "005c8e0e-15a2-595a-8bf4-9ff48deefe1b" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186238089 1081 "y=\\pm x" 13194139533392