6a6bf2-4c8f-5449-b6a1-92f670f185fc" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240437 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240786 1069 "q-103988" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240786 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240786 1071 "$\\int \\frac{2x+7}{(2x+1)(x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240786 1072 #uuid "ed315823-9912-53f6-ae66-2685405a1454" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240786 1074 "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+7}{(2x+1)(x+2)} & =\\frac{A}{2x+1}+\\frac{B}{x+2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n2x+7=A(x+2)+B(2x+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-2)+7 & =A((-2)+2)+B(2(-2)+1) \\\\ 3 & =-3B \\\\ B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-\\frac{1}{2})+7 & =A(-\\frac{1}{2}+2)+B(2(-\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\ 6 & =A(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+7}{(2x+1)(x+2)} & =\\frac{4}{2x+1}-\\frac{1}{x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+7}{(2x+1)(x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{4}{2x+1}-\\frac{1}{x+2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240787 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240480 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240787 1069 "q-103989" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240787 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240787 1071 "$\\int \\frac{2x-4}{x(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240787 1072 #uuid "992496e6-fae7-5b47-9d13-a4fe5d22fb68" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240787 1074 "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-4}{x(x-4)} & =\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x-4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n2x-4=A(x-4)+Bx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(4)-4 & =A(4-4)+B(4) \\\\ 4 & =4B \\\\ 1 & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-4 & =A(0-4)+B(0) \\\\ -4 & =-4A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-4}{x(x-4)} & =\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{x-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x-4}{x(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{x-4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240788 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240707 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240788 1069 "q-10399" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240788 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240788 1071 "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240788 1072 #uuid "d0a2cabe-af2e-5f4f-a8d3-773d2a0a8522" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240788 1074 "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\nx\\ne n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ This gives\n\n$$\nx-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\ne n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & \\ne n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne n\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240789 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240610 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240789 1069 "q-104020" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240789 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240789 1071 "![](images/4c/4c351342c55982f064955a311f9ea94220d58675e85c2e3514a12a8798f03df0.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240789 1072 #uuid "2df95855-c5ea-57bc-9c0a-5ac2a69badf8" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240789 1074 "The graph breaks at $x=0$ and $x=2,$ meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through these points.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=0$ and $x=2.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240790 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240680 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240790 1069 "q-104023" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240790 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240790 1071 "![](images/8e/8ee061e9348ada782791fca64a55923834dc87393518e9ec41c49698bfb2f92e.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240790 1072 #uuid "597a789c-a68c-549f-b2b0-c1130bde6299" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240790 1074 "The graph breaks at $x=-2$ and $x=0,$ meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through these points.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-2$ and $x=0.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240791 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240702 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240791 1069 "q-104040" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240791 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240791 1071 "Select the function that has a discontinuity at $x=-3.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240791 1072 #uuid "f110ad6d-6d4a-500c-a437-2aea9aeaa5ca" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240791 1074 "The correct answer is shown below. Notice that the function contains a break exactly at $x=-3$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nAll the other given graphs can be plotted without taking the pencil off of the paper." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240792 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240413 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240792 1069 "q-104049" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240792 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240792 1071 "![](images/51/5143df65d0f99c6511dc8ab13262831669c88535e19c986dacdb364e04847f58.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)\\ne f(2)$\n- $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240792 1072 #uuid "340a8e11-ca0d-5e08-ab08-b9647c00e4dc" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240792 1074 "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $f(2)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=2=f(2).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The graph of the function doesn't have a break at $x=2$ and we can draw the graph of $f(x)$ around $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240793 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240451 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240793 1069 "q-104051" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240793 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240793 1071 "![](images/65/65b9b32e8fac6a71141ce820855058367e9235129de7334db35a200905474cc0.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=5$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ exists\n- $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=5$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240793 1072 #uuid "fc9393f2-809c-5769-b329-186102da51c9" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240793 1074 "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(5)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}f(x) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,  ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ doesn't exist\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of the function has a jump exactly at $x=5$\nand it's impossible to draw the graph without taking our pencil off of the paper." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240794 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240664 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240794 1069 "q-104052" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240794 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240794 1071 "![](images/80/8086893e4587e68f8257b75f2070cf622fa141199d6bd8960f7c4fd8e7bcb349.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is **not** defined at $x=0$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)$ exists\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240794 1072 #uuid "3312f9fe-c2a1-5d75-b752-6c981a0a3f4b" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240794 1074 "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph we can see that no value has been assigned to $f(x)$ at $x=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x) & =3, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function is not even defined at $x=0.$ The graph contains a break at $x=0.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240795 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240706 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240795 1069 "q-104055" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240795 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240795 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}$ that satisfies $y(0)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240795 1072 #uuid "1086559f-da78-5256-be5a-8d4d9777777a" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240795 1074 "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =x+C \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =-x-C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-0-C \\\\ 0 & =0-C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-x\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240796 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240425 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240796 1069 "q-104058" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240796 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240796 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $(\\frac{1}{xy}-\\frac{1}{x})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2$ that satisfies $y(1)=1.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240796 1072 #uuid "cf490795-4587-5d21-a17e-b28b1511e0a5" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240796 1074 "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{xy}-\\frac{1}{x})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x}(\\frac{1}{y}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 \\\\ (\\frac{1}{y}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\int (\\frac{1}{y}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-y & ={x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(1)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1|-1 & ={1}^{2}+C \\\\ 0-1 & =1+C \\\\ C & =-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $(\\frac{1}{xy}-\\frac{1}{x})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=1$ in implicit form is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-y={x}^{2}-2\\,.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240797 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240407 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240797 1069 "q-104059" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240797 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240797 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+{y}^{2}$ that satisfies $y(-1)=1.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240797 1072 #uuid "5f5fc67b-8b37-59a0-97e4-36b78ca0108f" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240797 1074 "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =-\\frac{1}{x}+x+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =\\frac{1-{x}^{2}-Cx}{x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{1-{x}^{2}-Cx}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{1-{x}^{2}-Cx},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(-1)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(-1) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{-1}{1-(-1)^{2}-C(-1)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{-1}{C} & =1 \\\\ C & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+{y}^{2}$ that satisfies the condition $y(-1)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{1+x-{x}^{2}}\\,.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240798 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240512 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240798 1069 "q-10406" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240798 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240798 1071 "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+1?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240798 1072 #uuid "33ee86bf-83f2-58e4-8a42-08be20a94a04" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240798 1074 "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+1,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{3}\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{3} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240799 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240479 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240799 1069 "q-104064" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240799 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240799 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{3}$ that satisfies $y(2)=\\frac{1}{8}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240799 1072 #uuid "04d08db5-75c0-5a9e-a376-151cc06be633" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240799 1074 "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={y}^{2}(3{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (3{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =3\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & ={x}^{3}-{x}^{4}+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-C}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(2)=\\frac{1}{8}$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2) & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{2}^{4}-{2}^{3}-C} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8-C} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ C & =0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{3}$ that satisfies the condition $y(2)=\\frac{1}{8}$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}}\\,.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240800 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240561 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240800 1069 "q-104065" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240800 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240800 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ that satisfies $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={\\pi }^{2}.$ Assume that $0<x<\\pi .$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240800 1072 #uuid "cdddaa9b-4174-550d-a7b6-1b8e1a3f2b29" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240800 1074 "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+8x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x+8\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x+4{x}^{2}+{C}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the integral on the right-hand side of this equality, we use a substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+{C}_{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{C}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+4{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nwhere $C={C}_{1}+{C}_{2}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={\\pi }^{2}$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={\\pi }^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})+4{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+C & ={\\pi }^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1+{\\pi }^{2}+C & ={\\pi }^{2} \\\\ C & =0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ that satisfies the condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={\\pi }^{2}$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+4{x}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240801 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240348 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240801 1069 "q-104073" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240801 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240801 1071 "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x)=3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}$ at the point $(8,12)$ intersects the $y$-axis." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240801 1072 #uuid "9348de04-831f-5ba3-963b-71f2ffb24276" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240801 1074 "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2/3}) \\\\  & =2{x}^{-1/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(8)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{8}}=1,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-12 & =-(x-8) \\\\ y & =-x+8+12 \\\\ y & =20-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis by setting $x=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =20-0 \\\\ y & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $y$-axis at  $(0,20).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240802 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240758 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240802 1069 "q-104075" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240802 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240802 1071 "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}$ at the point $(1,2)$ intersects the $x$-axis." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240802 1072 #uuid "a4c51a1e-c57a-5c02-97b1-4b0a0b84934b" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240802 1074 "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{1/2} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=3\\sqrt{1}=3,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{1}{3}+2 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3} & =0 \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at  $(7,0).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240803 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240375 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240803 1069 "q-104119" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240803 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240803 1071 "Simplify the expression ${[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\theta }{5})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\theta }{5})]}^{20}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240803 1072 #uuid "ab4c66af-99f0-54ce-8f3e-d6fc71302f94" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240803 1074 "De Moivre's Theorem states that\n\n$$\n[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )]^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\phi )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=20$ and $\\phi =\\frac{2\\theta }{5}.$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\theta }{5})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\theta }{5})]}^{20} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(20\\cdot \\frac{2\\theta }{5})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(20\\cdot \\frac{2\\theta }{5}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240804 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240343 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240804 1069 "q-104122" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240804 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240804 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\frac{2}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ]^{4}}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240804 1072 #uuid "7fb50259-3b32-5ebe-a505-a65af7a2bda7" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240804 1074 "First, note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ]^{4}}=2[\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ]^{-4}.\n$$\n\nDe Moivre's Theorem states that\n\n$$\n[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi ]^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\phi )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=-4$ and $\\phi =2\\theta .$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ]^{4}} & =2[\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ]^{-4} \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(-4)\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(-4)\\theta )] \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-8\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-8\\theta )].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\phi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\phi )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )$ for any input $\\phi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n2[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-8\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-8\\theta )]=2[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\theta )].\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240805 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240613 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240805 1069 "q-104123" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240805 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240805 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\frac{1}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\theta ]^{3}}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240805 1072 #uuid "d84899af-756a-5c8a-9b35-3122383135e7" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240805 1074 "First, note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\theta ]^{3}}=[\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\theta ]^{-3}.\n$$\n\nDe Moivre's Theorem states that\n\n$$\n[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi ]^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\phi )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=-3$ and $\\phi =5\\theta .$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\theta ]^{3}} & =[\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\theta ]^{-3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(-3)\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5(-3)\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-15\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-15\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\phi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\phi )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )$ for any input $\\phi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-15\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-15\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(15\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(15\\theta ).\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240806 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240645 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240806 1069 "q-104124" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240806 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240806 1071 "![](images/bd/bdcfa35fb04028cbff32992b41214c878443d3815b676a0cf046f5a99bd771bd.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is **not** defined at $x=-2$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=f(-2)$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240806 1072 #uuid "ad98348d-cbe6-535a-9828-f9008c2ec2e3" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240806 1074 "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-2$ and $f(-2)=5.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x) & =5, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x) & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=5=f(-2).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of $f(x)$ can be drawn without taking our pencil off of the paper in the neighborhood of $x=-2.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240807 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240774 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240807 1069 "q-104129" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240807 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240807 1071 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=3?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{x-3}, & x<3 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{x-3}, & x>3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240807 1072 #uuid "cb39799c-0b6e-5be1-b620-9cc6473bd49a" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240807 1074 "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=3.$\n\n- We have that $f(3)$ is not defined since $x=3$ does not belong to the domain of $f(x).$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=3$ because $f(3)$ does not exist." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240808 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240785 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240808 1069 "q-104130" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240808 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240808 1071 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=-2?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{2}, & x<-2 \\\\ 4, & x=-2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+4}, & x>-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240808 1072 #uuid "51936c45-6840-50b5-8fa1-de7ee5753228" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240808 1074 "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- $f(-2)=4,$ so $f(-2)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)^{2}+4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+4}{2} \\\\  & =4, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-2)^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-2$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ does not exist." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240809 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240690 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240809 1069 "q-104132" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240809 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240809 1071 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=-2?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2}{2}, & x<-2 \\\\ 5, & x=-2 \\\\ \\frac{18}{{x}^{2}+2}, & x>-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240809 1072 #uuid "65ba7f63-40be-5e71-8f5e-9f52e8de1734" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240809 1074 "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- $f(-2)=5,$ so $f(-2)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and find that they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+2}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)^{2}+2}{2} \\\\  & =3, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}(\\frac{18}{{x}^{2}+2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{(-2)^{2}+2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=3.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=3,$ while $f(-2)=5.$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-2$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)\\ne f(-2).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240810 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240543 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240810 1069 "q-104134" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240810 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240810 1071 "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\ngiven that $det(A)=-1.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240810 1072 #uuid "0b281857-5cf5-5dda-b8cb-bae31f90bde6" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240810 1074 "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** We are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-1)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240811 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240663 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240811 1069 "q-104138" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240811 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240811 1071 "Find a vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ that parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z={e}^{xy}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240811 1072 #uuid "e095116a-4c29-5983-8d6b-40eb4ca9aa5f" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240811 1074 "A point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨x,\\,y,\\,{e}^{xy}⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain a vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,{e}^{uv}⟩\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240812 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240647 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240812 1069 "q-104140" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240812 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240812 1071 "Find a vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ that parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240812 1072 #uuid "83f27df2-5681-59a9-ab39-f8b0335df747" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240812 1074 "A point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain a vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1)⟩\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240813 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240638 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240813 1069 "q-104144" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240813 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240813 1071 "Find a vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ that parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=(x+y)^{4}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240813 1072 #uuid "01bb6202-1642-5ddf-82b8-f485aec63285" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240813 1074 "A point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨x,\\,y,\\,(x+y)^{4}⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain a vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,(u+v)^{4}⟩\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240814 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240384 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240814 1069 "q-104147" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240814 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240814 1071 "Find a vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ that parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=\\sqrt[3]{x-y}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240814 1072 #uuid "f7eea7ed-d51d-558b-9bfe-f151781f2286" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240814 1074 "A point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\sqrt[3]{x-y}⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain a vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\sqrt[3]{u-v}⟩\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240815 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240575 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240815 1069 "q-104148" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240815 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240815 1071 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=-\\sqrt{3}?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3}{3}, & x<-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3, & x=-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+3}, & x>-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240815 1072 #uuid "e2dc1316-63b7-56de-ae30-4568e3d25064" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240815 1074 "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- $f(-\\sqrt{3})=3,$ so $f(-\\sqrt{3})$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and find that they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{-}}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+3}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+3}{3} \\\\  & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{+}}(\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)=2.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)=2,$ while $f(-\\sqrt{3})=3.$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-\\sqrt{3}$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)\\ne f(-\\sqrt{3}).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240816 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240448 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240816 1069 "q-104151" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240816 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240816 1071 "$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{3}=0.$\n What is the third component of $r?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240816 1072 #uuid "7e5c15f1-3088-5f25-b189-55bc92394cd4" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240816 1074 "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{3} & =0 \\\\ z & =\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240817 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240400 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240817 1069 "q-104153" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240817 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240817 1071 "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=5$ and $f(5)=2,$ then what is the value of\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 5}\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-25f(x)}\\,?\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240817 1072 #uuid "8f6e92f8-d839-5c83-952d-4caeaa5ff806" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240817 1074 "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=5.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 5}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 5}f(x)=f(5)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 5}\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-25f(x)} & =\\sqrt{{5}^{3}-25\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{75} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240818 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240611 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240818 1069 "q-104155" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240818 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240818 1071 "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$ and $f(4)=-6,$ then what is ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(f(x))^{2}}{6x}?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240818 1072 #uuid "7d67c8d4-21f9-5990-9d5f-5827847125a8" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240818 1074 "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4)=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(f(x))^{2}}{6x} & =\\frac{(-6)^{2}}{6\\cdot 4}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240819 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240341 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240819 1069 "q-104162" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240819 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240819 1071 "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=7$ and $f(7)=13,$ then what is ${lim}_{x\\to {7}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-f(x))?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240819 1072 #uuid "6620dbb2-f094-5c7e-a736-3add7aafcfe7" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240819 1074 "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=7.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 7}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {7}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to 7}f(x)=f(7)=13.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {7}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-f(x)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{2}-13) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(36) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240820 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240345 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240820 1069 "q-104170" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240820 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240820 1071 "$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n${x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-{z}^{2}=1$\nfor $z>0.$ What is the third component of $r?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240820 1072 #uuid "9166ebb4-2524-528f-948b-ec0ea64eb1ca" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240820 1074 "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1 \\\\ z & =\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that $z>0.$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1}⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{3}-1}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{3}-1}.$\n\n**Note:** The parametrization is well-defined for $u$ and $v$ such that ${u}^{3}+{v}^{3}-1\\ge 0.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240821 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240700 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240821 1069 "q-104172" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240821 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240821 1071 "$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\text{_____}\\,k\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n${x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+1-{e}^{z}=0.$\n What is the third component of $r?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240821 1072 #uuid "77c5beb7-2be3-59ff-8b5f-6f45d544b913" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240821 1074 "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+1-{e}^{z} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{z} & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+1 \\\\ z & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=x\\,i+y\\,j+z\\,k=x\\,i+y\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+1)\\,k.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{2}+{v}^{4}+1)\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{2}+{v}^{4}+1).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240822 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240771 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240822 1069 "q-104179" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240822 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240822 1071 "$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n$\\frac{x+y}{6}+\\frac{z-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{6}.$\n What is the third component of $r?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240822 1072 #uuid "42a39179-0d26-58fa-a388-5b3e99abc6fb" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240822 1074 "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{6}+\\frac{z-1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{z-1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{x+y}{6} \\\\ \\frac{z-1}{2} & =\\frac{1-x-y}{6} \\\\ z & =\\frac{4-x-y}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\frac{4-x-y}{3}⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\frac{4-u-v}{3}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\frac{4-u-v}{3}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240823 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240454 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240823 1069 "q-10418" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240823 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240823 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-5{y}^{2}+8x+50y=129?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240823 1072 #uuid "505833c1-02e6-5b1d-b8f1-8ef1193de4ab" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240823 1074 "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2}+8x+50y & =129 \\\\ {x}^{2}+8x-5{y}^{2}+50y & =129 \\\\ {x}^{2}+8x-5[{y}^{2}-10y] & =129 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}-16-5[(y-5)^{2}-25] & =129 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}-16-5(y-5)^{2}+125 & =129 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}-5(y-5)^{2} & =129+16-125 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}-5(y-5)^{2} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{20}-\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center is $(-4,5).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240824 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240695 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240824 1069 "q-104181" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240824 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240824 1071 "$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\text{_____}\\,k\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n${x}^{2}y-{z}^{3}=3.$ \n What is the third component of $r?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240824 1072 #uuid "08e6d5b7-5e23-5057-9a31-e937d6bb0cdc" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240824 1074 "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y-{z}^{3} & =3 \\\\ {z}^{3} & ={x}^{2}y-3 \\\\ z & =\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}y-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=x\\,i+y\\,j+z\\,k=x\\,i+y\\,j+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}y-3}\\,k.\n$$\n\n   \n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}v-3}\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}v-3}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240825 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240696 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240825 1069 "q-104182" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240825 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240825 1071 "$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\text{_____}\\,k\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n$2x-3y+\\sqrt[3]{2z}=0.$\n What is the third component of $r?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240825 1072 #uuid "296ccbfc-3625-51a4-8c82-61c8a95bf002" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240825 1074 "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3y+\\sqrt[3]{2z} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{2z} & =3y-2x \\\\ z & =\\frac{(3y-2x)^{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=x\\,i+y\\,j+z\\,k=x\\,i+y\\,j+\\frac{(3y-2x)^{3}}{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\frac{(3v-2u)^{3}}{2}\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\frac{(3v-2u)^{3}}{2}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240826 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240516 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240826 1069 "q-10419" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240826 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240826 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola $4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-16y+4x=0?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240826 1072 #uuid "b8da39e7-a362-5791-804a-eb37d095c286" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240826 1074 "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-16y+4x & =0 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}-16y-{x}^{2}+4x & =0 \\\\ 4[{y}^{2}-4y]-[{x}^{2}-4x] & =0 \\\\ 4[(y-2)^{2}-4]-[(x-2)^{2}-4] & =0 \\\\ 4(y-2)^{2}-16-(x-2)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ 4(y-2)^{2}-(x-2)^{2} & =16-4 \\\\ 4(y-2)^{2}-(x-2)^{2} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{12} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center is $(2,2).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240827 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240403 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240827 1069 "q-104191" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240827 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240827 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $y=ln⁡x+\\frac{1}{6{x}^{2}}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240827 1072 #uuid "3c5fc6aa-a9f3-5065-aa63-827da84b30b5" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240827 1074 "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x+\\frac{1}{6{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x+\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-2){x}^{-2-1} \\\\  & ={x}^{-1}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-1}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =(-1){x}^{-1-1}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-3){x}^{-3-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240828 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240433 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240828 1069 "q-104192" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240828 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240828 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $y=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}+2sin⁡x.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240828 1072 #uuid "c7aef485-7834-5512-aabf-9615a3c07b9a" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240828 1074 "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}+2sin⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{-1/2}+2sin⁡x) \\\\  & =4(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{-1/2-1}+2cos⁡x \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-3/2}+2cos⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{x}^{-3/2}+2cos⁡x) \\\\  & =-2(-\\frac{3}{2}){x}^{-3/2-1}-2sin⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}-2sin⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240698 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240829 1069 "q-104204" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240829 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240829 1071 "![](images/bf/bfd160c9286927c9cafdf19fb591eba6b1c1a49ee0362c15c5a2fd26d50cdbc6.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240829 1072 #uuid "501921fb-f5aa-5b1d-9ef6-c871ad0ecd1b" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240829 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a circular cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis. \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- Since the distance from any point on our surface to the $z$-axis equals $1,$ we must have $r=1$ for every point on the surface.\n\n- On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $r=1.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240830 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240535 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240830 1069 "q-104208" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240830 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240830 1071 "![](images/24/2499715807dedd0234363454dea33afb592d497005c4f643b03145b6c89cc222.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240830 1072 #uuid "1bdd46c9-96f1-5f6a-ab6b-a539338b4af9" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240830 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis.\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- If we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $2$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. Therefore, we must have $\\theta =2$ for every point on the surface.\n\n- On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $\\theta =2.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240831 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240661 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240831 1069 "q-104209" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240831 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240831 1071 "![](images/e2/e2d50dc387d24ef2656adf0f6de402b5778416f6c82ce3d593fd0be8a777584a.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240831 1072 #uuid "f478bf92-d91e-5ac7-99c8-981da551fdec" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240831 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis.\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- If we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $\\frac{11\\pi }{8}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. Therefore, we must have $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{8}$ for every point on the surface.\n\n- On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{8}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240832 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240747 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240832 1069 "q-104213" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240832 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240832 1071 "![](images/fe/fe283d8f0d0b78729a7fc7bda93e900e1f310de2dbbc62f23d8f8a7ff65af462.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240832 1072 #uuid "ead21166-9605-5bc8-84ec-dbfb82a8902c" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240832 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a plane parallel to the $xy$-plane.\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- Since the $z$-coordinate of any point on our surface equals $-1,$ we must have $z=-1$ for every point on the surface.\n\n- On the other hand, $r$ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $z=-1.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240833 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240769 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240833 1069 "q-104214" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240833 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240833 1071 "![](images/c4/c4d6b6fe6b594192040f6dc6c3a37b7eacff4387dc31d2d9e5f353fefd5c4663.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240833 1072 #uuid "b59c20b1-4301-50d7-bd7e-48ab54116dca" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240833 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows the $xy$-plane.\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- Since the $z$-coordinate of any point on our surface equals $0,$ we must have $z=0$ for every point on the surface.\n\n- On the other hand, $r$ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $z=0.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240834 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240467 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240834 1069 "q-104219" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240834 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240834 1071 "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240834 1072 #uuid "5e8ed066-cedf-5856-9a34-59e242391831" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240834 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that if we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. So, we must have $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ for every point on the surface. On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, our surface is a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis, shown below.\n\n![](images/01/0118d67477cd0665d4ae5cbebbf3c3d6f3a6a24f4944977d4301cbaf99d3ecaa.png)" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240835 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240373 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240835 1069 "q-104225" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240835 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240835 1071 "What surface is defined by the equation $r=4$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240835 1072 #uuid "3d350350-c494-5683-bf54-5041494fa84d" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240835 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nr=4\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $z$-axis equals $4,$ while $\\theta $ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the circular cylinder shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc5c614a18a4cbaa50475c6612cc6b2c6458f9d5bed764b79ba52e624f1083c8.png)" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240836 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240773 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240836 1069 "q-104229" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240836 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240836 1071 "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240836 1072 #uuid "ede99d4b-4522-5a3b-b04a-ad1a84dcbbb8" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240836 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that if we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. So, we must have $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ for every point on the surface. On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, our surface is a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis, shown below.\n\n![](images/4e/4eefd7f719ccb0c34407d6c02ef1adeb283f45e80ed6a95525661b84e39b3a8d.png)" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240837 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240726 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240837 1069 "q-104231" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240837 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240837 1071 "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta =5$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240837 1072 #uuid "37abee3b-1032-56de-bf40-6b369a683c32" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240837 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =5\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that if we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $5$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. So, we must have $\\theta =5$ for every point on the surface. On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, our surface is a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis, shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7e2f3f2ad24c353bc3dc63d82971b4888ccffc198479456beddd7fea185bd07.png)" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240838 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240658 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240838 1069 "q-104240" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240838 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240838 1071 "What surface is defined by the equation ${z}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240838 1072 #uuid "af8f8ed9-eaf6-59bd-bd60-958037cbb5d2" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240838 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the square of the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals half the square of the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{z}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}}=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}}}=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the tangent of this angle can be a positive or negative number, the $z$-coordinate can also be positive or negative.\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the cone shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7c8d0cefa5eb8da76f6cba59570670e4078c278c801aeb12344dce3183733149.png)" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240839 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240401 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240839 1069 "q-104241" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240839 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240839 1071 "What surface is defined by the equation $z=\\frac{1}{5}r$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240839 1072 #uuid "0a63f55a-8c70-5d95-83cc-5a9cc3779061" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240839 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{1}{5}r\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals one-fifth the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{z}=\\frac{r}{(\\frac{1}{5}r)}=5.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the half-cone shown below.\n\n![](images/69/69978d3f1026cf83019924c44d66f0e510ce8c84b35d10c77c270b87809b30aa.png)" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240840 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240537 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240840 1069 "q-104243" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240840 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240840 1071 "![](images/44/446989d58605132a7e8e361b12127341c490845d893a792662b41c3d086e71e6.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240840 1072 #uuid "de4df632-8d7e-5bf5-a765-da66417b461a" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240840 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a circular half-cone whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis. \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- From the diagram, the angle between the position vector of any point on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nThe tangent of this angle equals the ratio between the distance $r$ from the point to the $z$-axis and the distance $z$ from the point to the $xy$-plane. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{z} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\frac{r}{z} & =1 \\\\ z & =r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $z=r.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240841 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240639 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240841 1069 "q-104244" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240841 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240841 1071 "![](images/ee/ee2764f5cf85f39107e458f347b048cc24227a55fae012b12f6cda4801cdfe78.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240841 1072 #uuid "773ba210-d4fa-5958-a8ac-1084763e8c5d" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240841 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a circular cone whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis. \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- From the diagram, the angle between the position vector of any point on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal $\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nThe tangent of this angle equals the ratio between the distance $r$ from the point to the $z$-axis and the distance $z$ from the point to the $xy$-plane. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{z} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\ \\frac{r}{z} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ z & =r\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain.\n\nFinally, recall that $r\\ge 0.$ Since we wish to include both half-cones, we need to include $z=-r\\sqrt{3}$ as well. We can combine the two equations by squaring both sides.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is ${z}^{2}=3{r}^{2}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240842 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240749 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240842 1069 "q-104270" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240842 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240842 1071 "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨\\text{_____},\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as \n$z=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the first component of $f?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240842 1072 #uuid "a3356c91-c06b-564a-ad7d-a325d54f849e" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240842 1074 "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $z=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain the parametrization \n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is  $r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240843 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240591 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240843 1069 "q-104273" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240843 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240843 1071 "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as \n$\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{r}^{4}}={sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta $\nfor $z\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ What is the third component of $f?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240843 1072 #uuid "ab4208a8-9578-51f0-acb9-055b5b3297cd" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240843 1074 "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(r,\\theta ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{r}^{4}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta  \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={r}^{4}{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta  \\\\ z & ={r}^{2}|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta | \\\\ z & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that $z\\ge 0.$ Also, we drop the absolute value bars because $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\ge 0$ for  $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$  \n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $z={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is ${r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta .$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240844 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240521 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240844 1069 "q-104276" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240844 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240844 1071 "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as \n$z=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta .$\nWhat is the second component of $f?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240844 1072 #uuid "1faacb70-5b28-5bb1-8a52-fcbfb9493171" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240844 1074 "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $z=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta ,$ we obtain the parametrization\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta ⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240845 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240517 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240845 1069 "q-104278" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240845 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240845 1071 "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨\\text{_____},\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as \n$z=2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta .$\nWhat is the first component of $f?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240845 1072 #uuid "be8626dd-33da-5043-a0a7-46061d17b29a" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240845 1074 "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $z=2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ,$ we obtain the parametrization \n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240846 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240646 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240846 1069 "q-104283" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240846 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240846 1071 "If $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-5$ and ${lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{+}}f(x)=3,$ then what is ${lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{+}}(f(x)-2f(-5))?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240846 1072 #uuid "5f74b17a-b71c-5fd1-b977-bfbf3cab75b5" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240846 1074 "Since $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-5,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{+}}f(x)=f(-5)=3.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{+}}(f(x)-2f(-5)) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{+}}f(x)-2f(-5) \\\\  & =3-2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240847 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240621 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240847 1069 "q-104285" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240847 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240847 1071 "If $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=-9$ and $f(-9)=4,$ then what is \n${lim}_{x\\to (-9)^{-}}(f(x)-x)^{2}?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240847 1072 #uuid "805195e4-c372-59d7-a8e0-d0c12e8b1750" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240847 1074 "Since $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=-9,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-9)^{-}}f(x)=f(-9)=4.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-9)^{-}}(f(x)-x)^{2}=(4-(-9))^{2}=169.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240848 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240528 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240848 1069 "q-104286" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240848 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240848 1071 "If $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=-1$ and ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=-6,$ then what is \n${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}(2f(x)-xf(-1))?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240848 1072 #uuid "6fee77e9-2f42-55d5-b9e7-c63e3157522b" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240848 1074 "Since $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=f(-1)=-6.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}(2f(x)-xf(-1)) & =2{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)-f(-1){lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}x \\\\  & =2(-6)-(-6)(-1) \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240849 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240578 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240849 1069 "q-104290" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240849 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240849 1071 "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as \n$4{r}^{2}-{z}^{2}=0$\nfor $z\\ge 0.$ What is the third component of $f?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240849 1072 #uuid "50194062-3876-55b3-9db5-9f902647cbd5" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240849 1074 "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(r,\\theta ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =4{r}^{2} \\\\ z & =\\sqrt{4{r}^{2}} \\\\ z & =2r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that $z\\ge 0.$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $z=2r,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $2r.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240339 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240850 1069 "q-104294" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240850 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240850 1071 "Given $f(x)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-8\\sqrt{{x}^{5}},$ find ${f}^{‴}(x).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240850 1072 #uuid "dbc15ca0-e95e-5ce8-90b9-fc14fcd67fee" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240850 1074 "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-8\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-8{x}^{5/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}-20{x}^{3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{x}-20{x}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-1}-20{x}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-2}-30{x}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{x}^{-2}-30{x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =4{x}^{-3}-15{x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240851 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240782 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240851 1069 "q-104297" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240851 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240851 1071 "Differentiate ${e}^{x}cos⁡x.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240851 1072 #uuid "6d7240cf-a773-5ae8-8c7e-03955317789e" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240851 1074 "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=cos⁡x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({e}^{x}cos⁡x)^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})^{′}\\cdot cos⁡x+{e}^{x}\\cdot (cos⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(cos⁡x)+({e}^{x})(-sin⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}cos⁡x-{e}^{x}sin⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240852 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240599 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240852 1069 "q-1043" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240852 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240852 1071 "Find ${Y}^{T}$, given that \n    $Y=[\\begin{aligned}-20 & 0 & 15 \\\\ 9 & 6 & 0.5\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240852 1072 #uuid "08ffa811-ae94-5628-bc33-bfa31f978aaf" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240852 1074 "To find the transpose, we interchange the rows and columns:\n\n$$\n{Y}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-20 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 6 \\\\ 15 & 0.5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240853 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240688 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240853 1069 "q-10431" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240853 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240853 1071 "Find the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}=1.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240853 1072 #uuid "a749a329-adce-56a3-ac55-3f1f3df20656" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240853 1074 "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na=\\sqrt{4}=2,\\,b=\\sqrt{16}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{2}{4}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240854 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240376 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240854 1069 "q-104325" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240854 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240854 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ if $y=\\frac{3sec⁡x}{{e}^{x}}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240854 1072 #uuid "a8b729c6-447d-5486-b8ab-6761a892119d" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240854 1074 "We calculate the derivative using the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3sec⁡x}{{e}^{x}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(3sec⁡xtan⁡x)({e}^{x})-(3sec⁡x)({e}^{x})}{({e}^{x})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{x}sec⁡x(tan⁡x-1)}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3sec⁡x(tan⁡x-1)}{{e}^{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240855 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240534 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240855 1069 "q-104329" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240855 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240855 1071 "![](images/a8/a89469b49ecb4f40ba8378e1ce5404f5a1cf0eb4e78922529d9dce368b28c82d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240855 1072 #uuid "11d1bb30-6c4f-5361-a860-88f0d5525276" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240855 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a circular cone whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis. \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- From the diagram, the angle between the position vector of any point on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nThe tangent of this angle equals the ratio between the distance $r$ from the point to the $z$-axis and the distance $z$ from the point to the $xy$-plane. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{z} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\frac{r}{z} & =1 \\\\ z & =r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain.\n\nFinally, recall that $r\\ge 0.$ Since we wish to include both half-cones, we need to include $z=-r$ as well. We can combine the two equations by squaring both sides.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is ${z}^{2}={r}^{2}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240856 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240616 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240856 1069 "q-104330" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240856 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240856 1071 "The quadratic polynomial $P(x)$ has the double root ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=9.$ Find $P(x).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240856 1072 #uuid "a2d13fab-7a95-5b38-9295-5edf9dd98e58" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240856 1074 "Since our quadratic polynomial has the double root ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=9,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})^{2} & =9{(x-(-\\frac{2}{3}))}^{2} \\\\  & =9{(x+\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{(x+\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} & =9({x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}x+\\frac{4}{9}) \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}+12x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=9{x}^{2}+12x+4.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240857 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240551 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240857 1069 "q-104335" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240857 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240857 1071 "What surface is defined by the equation $z=3r$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240857 1072 #uuid "301f75d0-c5ba-5a32-b21e-ff6181a24922" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240857 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=3r\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals $3$ times the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{z}=\\frac{r}{3r}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the half-cone shown below.\n\n![](images/69/69978d3f1026cf83019924c44d66f0e510ce8c84b35d10c77c270b87809b30aa.png)" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240858 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240513 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240858 1069 "q-104343" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240858 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240858 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\frac{6}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ]^{5}}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240858 1072 #uuid "eba0abc0-d36a-5439-b495-fad6bb86a003" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240858 1074 "First, note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ]^{5}}=[\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ]^{-5}.\n$$\n\nDe Moivre's Theorem states that\n\n$$\n[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi ]^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\phi )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=-5$ and $\\phi =3\\theta .$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ]^{5}} & =6[\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ]^{-5} \\\\  & =6[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(-5)\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(-5)\\theta )] \\\\  & =6[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-15\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-15\\theta )].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\phi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\phi )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )$ for any input $\\phi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n6[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-15\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-15\\theta )]=6[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(15\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(15\\theta )].\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240859 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240430 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240859 1069 "q-104345" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240859 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240859 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\frac{2}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}^{12}}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240859 1072 #uuid "3ca9f116-e0af-5a75-8a27-7b1496ad0d00" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240859 1074 "First, note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}^{12}}={[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}^{-12}.\n$$\n\nDe Moivre's Theorem states that\n\n$$\n{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )]}^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\phi )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=-12$ and $\\phi =\\theta .$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}^{12}} & =2{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}^{-12} \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-12\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-12\\theta )].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\phi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\phi )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )$ for any input $\\phi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n2[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-12\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-12\\theta )]=2[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12\\theta )].\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240860 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240475 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240860 1069 "q-10435" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240860 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240860 1071 "Find the asymptotes of the hyperbola ${y}^{2}-9{x}^{2}=9.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240860 1072 #uuid "6e943e64-b5a5-5bd1-b617-3b622a923d3b" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240860 1074 "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the given equation in standard form, we divide both sides of the equation by $9$ and then simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-9{x}^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{9}{9} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $a=\\sqrt{9}=3$ and $b=\\sqrt{1}=1.$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{3}{1}x \\\\  & =\\pm 3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240861 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240684 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240861 1069 "q-104357" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240861 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240861 1071 "![](images/30/30df82b4c47905fd8277067f9207a5e970a96d8ec386879d2872b5f5c3d8dd3c.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- The function is left-continuous at $x=-1$\n- The function is right-continuous at $x=-1$\n- The function is continuous at $x=-1$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240861 1072 #uuid "d00cc33e-d21d-57ae-828a-16fa83891b51" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240861 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=1\\ne 0=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=-1\\ne 0=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240862 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240525 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240862 1069 "q-104358" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240862 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240862 1071 "![](images/7c/7c95fb061d6b860655e934f2691d03e53f7bb77a617200f523f1695ee49083fe.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- The function is left-continuous at $x=1$\n- The function is right-continuous at $x=1$\n- The function is continuous at $x=1$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240862 1072 #uuid "fc756662-eff5-518b-9ac2-39ac467a0419" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240862 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=1,$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=4=f(1)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false.  The function is not right-continuous at $x=1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=2\\ne 4=f(1)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement  I is true." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240863 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240469 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240863 1069 "q-104365" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240863 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240863 1071 "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{ _____ }⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as \n$ϕ=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the third component of $f?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240863 1072 #uuid "858c0510-e516-5f4a-9f59-887279f0bad1" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240863 1074 "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $\\rho =f(\\theta ,ϕ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\rho  & =\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho =\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $ϕ\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240864 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240687 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240864 1069 "q-104368" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240864 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240864 1071 "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as \n$\\rho =ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the first component of $f?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240864 1072 #uuid "0182a635-c5bf-5468-bfb5-8e21b55ef8c2" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240864 1074 "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho =ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨ϕ{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $ϕ{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240865 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240337 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240865 1069 "q-104372" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240865 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240865 1071 "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as \n$\\rho =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the first component of $f?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240865 1072 #uuid "a9e5109e-4f42-5a9d-b1d9-a120a5c5fa29" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240865 1074 "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240866 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240505 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240866 1069 "q-104375" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240866 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240866 1071 "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as \n$\\rho =ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the second component of $f?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240866 1072 #uuid "f7322663-d17a-5b8b-9446-b90394808a49" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240866 1074 "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho =ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $ϕ{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240650 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240867 1069 "q-104378" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240867 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240867 1071 "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{ _____ },\\,ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as \n$ϕ=\\rho \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the first component of $f?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240867 1072 #uuid "d129949e-0a68-5849-90cb-ed7f8e4ee14f" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240867 1074 "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $\\rho =f(\\theta ,ϕ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\rho \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\rho  & =ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho =ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240868 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240590 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240868 1069 "q-10438" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240868 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240868 1071 "Find the asymptotes of the hyperbola $25{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}=100.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240868 1072 #uuid "3b867116-3bc8-5753-8c33-6fa2ab2e608f" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240868 1074 "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the given equation in standard form, we divide both sides of the equation by $100$ and then simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2} & =100 \\\\ \\frac{25{y}^{2}}{100}-\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{100} & =\\frac{100}{100} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $a=\\sqrt{4}=2$ and $b=\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\frac{2}{5}x.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240869 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240394 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240869 1069 "q-104384" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240869 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240869 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3},\\,\\, & x<2 \\\\ 8,\\,\\, & x=2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2},\\, & x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240869 1072 #uuid "23c6c497-7b53-5892-b312-12905adbf824" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240869 1074 "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $f(x)$ at $x=2.$\n\nFirst, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=2$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={2}^{3} \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & =f(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=2$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =2(2)^{2} \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & =f(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=2,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240870 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240410 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240870 1069 "q-104387" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240870 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240870 1071 "Which of the following functions has a removable discontinuity at $x=-1?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240870 1072 #uuid "40e45536-522a-5497-88c6-6cdced7f0efd" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240870 1074 "The function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-1)=-3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1)$ $\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-1$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\n![](images/34/340443e20bc285d2e711b81608a60eb81ff33537ece23860b11871f5373dfde2.png)\n\nNotice that the functions $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are continuous at $x=-1,$ while $g(x)$ and $h(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=-1$ but they are *not* removable." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240871 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240730 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240871 1069 "q-104396" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240871 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240871 1071 "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y$ a potential function?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240871 1072 #uuid "2f3ac565-e5b1-59a8-9f54-0c7e96f05c2f" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240871 1074 "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i-j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=2x\\,i-j.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240872 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240536 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240872 1069 "q-104397" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240872 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240872 1071 "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ a potential function?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240872 1072 #uuid "139d3292-e475-5138-868b-b4cb7235237f" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240872 1074 "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})j \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240873 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240600 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240873 1069 "q-104398" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240873 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240873 1071 "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-3y)$ a potential function?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240873 1072 #uuid "cbb95ba6-f378-5cdd-befa-8f5b533b38d2" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240873 1074 "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-3y))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-3y))\\,j \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240874 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240672 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240874 1069 "q-104407" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240874 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240874 1071 "![](images/03/039c88a5cb4278b5522ccb95cde8a92f48ae572deb3ad38845c2ceaaec1e90ac.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above. For what values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x)$ have a removable discontinuity?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240874 1072 #uuid "1273d810-faf3-5cdd-b274-f0de7af4b386" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240874 1074 "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)=-4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=-2,$ but it is not a point removable discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(-2)=-2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=-2$ is not a point removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=2$ only." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240875 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240712 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240875 1069 "q-10441" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240875 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240875 1071 "Find the asymptotes of the hyperbola $25{y}^{2}-16{x}^{2}=400.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240875 1072 #uuid "7b8ae9c2-fb69-5bfa-b14f-e369229e7d62" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240875 1074 "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the given equation in standard form, we divide both sides of the equation by $400$ and then simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{y}^{2}-16{x}^{2} & =400 \\\\ \\frac{25{y}^{2}}{400}-\\frac{16{x}^{2}}{400} & =\\frac{400}{400} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $a=\\sqrt{16}=4$ and $b=\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\frac{4}{5}x.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240876 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240566 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240876 1069 "q-104410" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240876 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240876 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=(ay-2x{y}^{3})\\,i+(x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\,j$ on ${R}^{2},$ where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240876 1072 #uuid "f05e7a0d-26f2-588a-8d78-a6a57d732300" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240876 1074 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ we have $P=ay-2x{y}^{3}$ and $Q=x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=a-6x{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1-6x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-6x{y}^{2} & =1-6x{y}^{2} \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=1.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240877 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240340 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240877 1069 "q-104413" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240877 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240877 1071 "Determine the entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240877 1072 #uuid "fc11b8c2-85be-59ad-bbca-214a365781c5" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240877 1074 "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}A=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =3|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(1-0)-0+(-1-0) \\\\  & =3+0-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{23}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{32}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240878 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240772 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240878 1069 "q-104414" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240878 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240878 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=\\frac{axy}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2},$ where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240878 1072 #uuid "7bd6d6aa-1b16-5a93-a052-cdf54093004e" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240878 1074 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ we have $P=\\frac{axy}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and $Q=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{ax}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{ax}{{x}^{2}+1} & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ ax & =2x \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=2.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240879 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240365 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240879 1069 "q-104415" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240879 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240879 1071 "![](images/80/8050047cf4381ef77d5569ac23b2adaa6b8484f5be672fd9a52bf011beeb39e8.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above. For what values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x)$ have a removable discontinuity?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240879 1072 #uuid "364a4d91-4151-5e47-aa9a-aa47376fefdb" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240879 1074 "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-1)=2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=-3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-1$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function also has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(3)=-2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=3$ is also a removable discontinuity.\n\nFinally,  the function has a discontinuity at $x=5.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(5)$ does not exists $\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the first condition that failed, which tells us that $x=5$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=-1$ and $x=3$ only." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240880 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240352 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240880 1069 "q-104416" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240880 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240880 1071 "Determine the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240880 1072 #uuid "d6189c9e-c299-5fe1-934e-c85b7854108f" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240880 1074 "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}A=|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =(-1)|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(2+1)-(1-2)+0 \\\\  & =-3+1+0 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{13}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{13} & =(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1-4 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{31}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13}=\\frac{1}{-2}\\cdot (-5)=\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240881 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240483 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240881 1069 "q-104419" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240881 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240881 1071 "![](images/ae/ae097e5f002d970ffe7393cacce708d431f4ef1f46c5a2c95e3fa1f5846d2c7d.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above. For what values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x)$ have a removable discontinuity?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240881 1072 #uuid "f47957f4-3523-5a9d-a877-a00c8d275b5a" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240881 1074 "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(2)=-1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function also has a discontinuity at $x=4.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(4)=-3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=-1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)\\ne f(4)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=4$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(1)=3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=-1$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nFinally, the function has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(3)=2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=3$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=2$ and $x=4$ only." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240882 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240523 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240882 1069 "q-104428" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240882 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240882 1071 "The entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ k & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $det(A)=2k+2.$ Find the value of $k.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240882 1072 #uuid "57a186fd-b1ba-539f-bb9d-e1998b570fc0" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240882 1074 "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=2k+2\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(0-2) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2k+2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{12}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{k+1} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ k+1 & =2 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240883 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240497 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240883 1069 "q-104431" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240883 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240883 1071 "The entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & k \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $-1$ and $det(A)=4-2k.$ Find the value of $k.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240883 1072 #uuid "539f41b1-227f-56ae-b693-cf3443bf466c" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240883 1074 "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=4-2k\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{23} & =(-1)^{2+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(2-0) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{32}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-2k}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{32}^{′}=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{k-2} & =-1 \\\\ 1 & =2-k \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240470 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240884 1069 "q-104439" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240884 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240884 1071 "The entry ${a}_{22}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & k & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $det(A)=k-4.$ Find the value of $k.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240884 1072 #uuid "55417378-dec5-552f-8b04-ebd15181a9f3" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240884 1074 "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=k-4\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{22}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{22}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{22} & =(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{22}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{22} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k-4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{22}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{4}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{k-4} \\\\ k-4 & =-4 \\\\ k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240378 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240885 1069 "q-104442" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240885 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240885 1071 "Find the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ if $f(x)=\\frac{sec⁡x}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\pi }.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240885 1072 #uuid "10b5a865-9dfd-5753-8931-5381fe6e32af" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240885 1074 "First, we take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{sec⁡x}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{sec⁡xtan⁡x}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{6x}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ in the derivative and get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{\\pi /3} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot sec⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot tan⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\frac{6}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\sqrt{3}+2 \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240886 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240733 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240886 1069 "q-104446" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240886 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240886 1071 "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=4$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $2\\pi $ | $-3$ | $\\pi $ | $5$ |\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))$ at $x=4.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240886 1072 #uuid "c4b0b50f-90d4-5470-b683-6843a3c78aaf" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240886 1074 "Using the product rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))=f(x){g}^{′}(x)+g(x){f}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=4,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=4} & =f(4){g}^{′}(4)+g(4){f}^{′}(4) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot 5+(-3)\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =7\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240887 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240741 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240887 1069 "q-104447" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240887 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240887 1071 "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you're given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(-7)=2\\sqrt{3},\\,{f}^{′}(-7)=\\sqrt{3},\\,g(-7)=5\\sqrt{3}\\,{g}^{′}(-7)=-4\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))$ at $x=-7.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240887 1072 #uuid "65776d17-efb8-5e62-9acc-c1345c63bc04" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240887 1074 "Using the product rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))=f(x){g}^{′}(x)+g(x){f}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=-7,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=-7} & =f(-7){g}^{′}(-7)+g(-7){f}^{′}(-7) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-4\\sqrt{3})+5\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240888 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240429 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240888 1069 "q-104453" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240888 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240888 1071 "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=-6$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-6$ | $-2\\pi $ | $3\\pi $ | $5$ | $-2$ |\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)+2g(x))$ at $x=-6.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240888 1072 #uuid "d1d55ff1-19e4-58c5-a23a-1cef5eceb55b" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240888 1074 "Using the sum and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)+2g(x))={f}^{′}(x)+2{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=-6$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)+2g(x))|}_{x=-6} & ={f}^{′}(-6)+2{g}^{′}(-6) \\\\  & =5+2(-2) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240889 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240660 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240889 1069 "q-104458" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240889 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240889 1071 "The cubic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=-3,$ ${x}_{2}=-2,$ and ${x}_{3}=3,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $4.$ Find $P(x).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240889 1072 #uuid "8fee6d49-5312-52da-a5d1-9f9f37f07d8e" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240889 1074 "Since our cubic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}=-3,$ ${x}_{1}=-2,$ and ${x}_{3}=3,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=4,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) & =4(x-(-3))(x-(-2))(x-3) \\\\  & =4(x+3)(x+2)(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x+3)(x+2)(x-3) & =4({x}^{2}+5x+6)(x-3) \\\\  & =4({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+5{x}^{2}-15x+6x-18) \\\\  & =4({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-9x-18) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}-36x-72.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=4{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}-36x-72.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240890 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240617 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240890 1069 "q-104462" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240890 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240890 1071 "The cubic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=2,{x}_{2}={x}_{3}=4,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $-5.$ Find $P(x).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240890 1072 #uuid "6122ecf9-65cc-59e2-933c-fdca047ffeb1" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240890 1074 "Since our cubic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}=4,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=-5,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) & =-5(x-2)(x-4)(x-4) \\\\  & =-5(x-2)(x-4)(x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5(x-2)(x-4)(x-4) & =-5({x}^{2}-6x+8)(x-4) \\\\  & =-5({x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2}+24x+8x-32) \\\\  & =-5({x}^{3}-10{x}^{2}+32x-32) \\\\  & =-5{x}^{3}+50{x}^{2}-160x+160.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-5{x}^{3}+50{x}^{2}-160x+160.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240891 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240544 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240891 1069 "q-104467" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240891 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240891 1071 "The cubic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=-5,$ ${x}_{2}=-4,$ and ${x}_{3}=-3,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $-1.$ Find $P(x).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240891 1072 #uuid "6d1aea48-9d5c-5087-9417-75755eb817eb" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240891 1074 "Since our cubic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}=-5,$ ${x}_{1}=-4,$ and ${x}_{3}=-3,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=-1,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) & =-1(x-(-5))(x-(-4))(x-(-3)) \\\\  & =-(x+5)(x+4)(x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(x+5)(x+4)(x+3) & =-({x}^{2}+9x+20)(x+3) \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+9{x}^{2}+27x+20x+60) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-47x-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-47x-60.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240892 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240495 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240892 1069 "q-104471" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240892 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240892 1071 "The cubic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=-3,{x}_{3}=1,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $5.$ Find $P(x).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240892 1072 #uuid "0630788c-05ca-5842-a002-ad75027a57f8" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240892 1074 "Since our cubic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=-3$ and ${x}_{3}=1,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=5,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) & =5(x-(-3))(x-(-3))(x-1) \\\\  & =5(x+3)(x+3)(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(x+3)(x+3)(x-1) & =5({x}^{2}+6x+9)(x-1) \\\\  & =5({x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+6{x}^{2}-6x+9x-9) \\\\  & =5({x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+3x-9) \\\\  & =5{x}^{3}+25{x}^{2}+15x-45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=5{x}^{3}+25{x}^{2}+15x-45.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240893 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240372 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240893 1069 "q-104478" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240893 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240893 1071 "![](images/8d/8d940d158c631143a1fe7652a4fb83bf2f67f3fd4bbdbbc4a33643bd282b5028.png)\n\nThe Argand diagram above shows the roots of the cubic polynomial $f(x).$ Find $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the cubic term is $4.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240893 1072 #uuid "a3f1939b-50de-5b94-bcb7-196c51156f21" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240893 1074 "From the diagram, we see that $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=0$ and ${x}_{2,3}=-1\\pm 2\\text{i}.$ Additionally, we're given that the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=4.$ Therefore, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =4(x-0)(x-(-1+2\\text{i}))(x-(-1-2\\text{i})) \\\\  & =4x(x-(-1+2\\text{i}))(x-(-1-2\\text{i}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x(x-(-1+2\\text{i}))(x-(-1-2\\text{i})) & = \\\\ 4x({x}^{2}-x(-1-2\\text{i})-x(-1+2\\text{i})+(-1+2\\text{i})(-1-2\\text{i})) & = \\\\ 4x({x}^{2}+2x+5) & = \\\\ 4{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}+20x & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=4{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}+20x.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240894 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240567 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240894 1069 "q-104487" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240894 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240894 1071 "The cubic polynomial $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=-5,{x}_{2,3}=\\pm 3\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $3.$ Find $f(x).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240894 1072 #uuid "a483d6da-3355-5ef5-a0a8-57b7cc6e7d69" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240894 1074 "Since $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=-5$ and ${x}_{2,3}=\\pm 3\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=3,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =3(x-(-5))(x-3\\text{i})(x-(-3\\text{i})) \\\\  & =3(x+5)(x-3\\text{i})(x+3\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x+5)(x-3\\text{i})(x+3\\text{i}) & = \\\\ 3(x+5)({x}^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ 3(x+5)({x}^{2}+9) & = \\\\ 3({x}^{3}+9x+5{x}^{2}+45) & = \\\\ 3{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}+27x+135 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}+27x+135.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240895 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240472 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240895 1069 "q-104489" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240895 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240895 1071 "The cubic polynomial $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=-2,{x}_{2,3}=3\\pm \\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $-1.$ Find $f(x).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240895 1072 #uuid "af9df84b-0467-5970-802a-2be3fc5449e7" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240895 1074 "Since $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=-2$ and ${x}_{2,3}=3\\pm \\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=-1,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-1(x-(-2))(x-(3+\\text{i}))(x-(3-\\text{i})) \\\\  & =-(x+2)(x-(3+\\text{i}))(x-(3-\\text{i}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(x+2)(x-(3+\\text{i}))(x-(3-\\text{i})) & = \\\\ -(x+2)({x}^{2}-x(3-\\text{i})-x(3+\\text{i})+(3+\\text{i})(3-\\text{i})) & = \\\\ -(x+2)({x}^{2}-6x+10) & = \\\\ -({x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+10x+2{x}^{2}-12x+20) & = \\\\ -{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+2x-20 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+2x-20.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240896 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240572 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240896 1069 "q-104497" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240896 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240896 1071 "Given that $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is a solution to the equation $3{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+8x-2=0,$ find the remaining solutions." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240896 1072 #uuid "f1228222-7a6a-50f0-87dd-428760eee980" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240896 1074 "Let $f(x)=9{x}^{3}-21{x}^{2}+24x-6.$ Since $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x-\\frac{1}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x-\\frac{1}{3})$ using synthetic division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\enclose{bottom right}{\\frac{1}{3}} & 3 & -7 & 8 & -2 \\\\  &  & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\  & 3 & -6 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=(x-\\frac{1}{3})(3{x}^{2}-6x+6).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $3{x}^{2}-6x+6=0$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x+6 & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-2x+2) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+2 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =(\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=\\frac{1}{3},\\,1\\pm \\text{i}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240897 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240751 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240897 1069 "q-104514" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240897 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240897 1071 "Find all of the solutions to ${x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-2x+20=0$ given that $x=3-\\text{i}$ is a solution." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240897 1072 #uuid "0788e647-7a4e-5549-adea-a1cd056a2776" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240897 1074 "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-2x+20.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=3-\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{3-\\text{i}}=3+\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-(3-\\text{i}))(x-(3+\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(3-\\text{i}))(x-(3+\\text{i})) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-(3-\\text{i}+(3+\\text{i)})x+(3-\\text{i})(3+\\text{i}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+({3}^{2}-(\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+10 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-6x+10$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x+2 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-6x+10\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-2x+20} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2{x}^{2}-12x+20 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}-12x+20}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n     \n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-6x+10)(x+2).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2=0\\,⟹\\,x=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=-2,3\\pm \\text{i}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240898 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240455 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240898 1069 "q-104516" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240898 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240898 1071 "Find all of the solutions to ${x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-11x-116=0$ given that $x=-5+2\\text{i}$ is a solution." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240898 1072 #uuid "d1be54b7-7530-59ba-90a4-e2866a7b30e9" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240898 1074 "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-11x-116.$\n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=-5+2\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{-5+2\\text{i}}=-5-2\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-(-5+2\\text{i}))(x-(-5-2\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(-5+2\\text{i}))(x-(-5-2\\text{i})) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-(-5+2\\text{i}+(-5-2\\text{i)})x+(-5+2\\text{i})(-5-2\\text{i}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+10x+((-5)^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+10x+29 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}+10x+29$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x-4 \\\\  & {x}^{2}+10x+29\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-11x-116} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}+29x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,-4{x}^{2}+40x-116 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,{-4{x}^{2}+40x-116}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n        \n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}+10x+29)(x-4).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-4=0\\,⟹\\,x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=4,-5\\pm 2\\text{i}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240899 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240444 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240899 1069 "q-104536" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240899 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240899 1071 "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y+z=1 \\\\ 2x-2z=2+2y \\\\ 2y+4z=2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind  $x+y+z.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240899 1072 #uuid "b86d16b9-91e7-5d11-97b1-e0f931afe9d2" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240899 1074 "First, let's rewrite the system in the correct order:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y+z=1 \\\\ 2x-2y-2z=2 \\\\ -2x+2y+4z=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we  write the system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-(8-4)+(4-4) \\\\  & =-4+0 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{-4}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to the given system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x+y+z=2+1+0=3.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240900 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240354 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240900 1069 "q-104541" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240900 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240900 1071 "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}y+z+x=2 \\\\ -3z+y=3 \\\\ 2x+y=4-4z\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240900 1072 #uuid "30372d4a-7cb0-560b-a8cf-a404cabf96f7" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240900 1074 "We organize the system so that it’s in t