nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=11,y=-6,z=-3.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240901 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240710 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240901 1069 "q-104546" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240901 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240901 1071 "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}z+x=2 \\\\ 2y-3z=1 \\\\ 2z-y=-3x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240901 1072 #uuid "5c5fa419-7d88-586a-9224-2f097b998fb5" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240901 1074 "We organize the system so that it’s in the correct order first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x+z=2 \\\\ 2y-3z=1 \\\\ 3x-y+2z=3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(4-3)-0+(0-6) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -9 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -9 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -9 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -9 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=1,y=2,z=1.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240902 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240379 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240902 1069 "q-10457" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240902 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240902 1071 "$\\int (5{cos}^{2}⁡2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240902 1072 #uuid "61dee2fb-b4a6-546d-85eb-f80ded427acb" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240902 1074 "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =2x$, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡2x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5{cos}^{2}⁡2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int [5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1)-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\frac{5}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\frac{5x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240903 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240396 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240903 1069 "q-104572" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240903 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240903 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x+11}, & x<5 \\\\ 12, & x=5 \\\\ {e}^{5/x}, & x>5\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements is true?\n\n- $f(5)$ exists\n- ${lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}f(x)$\n- $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=5$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240903 1072 #uuid "8b8ec7f0-866e-5db4-b2de-0c9d1fe0bd7c" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240903 1074 "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $f(5)=12$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true.\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}\\sqrt{x+11}=4, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}{e}^{5/x}=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that statement II is false and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}f(x).\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSince the second condition failed, $x=5$ is not a removable discontinuity. So, statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240904 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240478 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240904 1069 "q-104573" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240904 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240904 1071 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F=(2x-y)\\,i-x\\,j$\n- $G=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,j$\n- $H={e}^{x-y}\\,i-{e}^{x-y}\\,j$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240904 1072 #uuid "7ff94cca-f0c0-5b4f-9e96-5ccdc57e1837" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240904 1074 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=2x-y$ and $Q=-x.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$ and $Q=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P={e}^{x-y}$ and $Q=-{e}^{x-y}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{e}^{x-y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-{e}^{x-y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240905 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240632 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240905 1069 "q-104575" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240905 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240905 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-34, & x<-3 \\\\ -25, & x=-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}+2, & x>-3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}g(x)$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)=g(-3)$\n- $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-3$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240905 1072 #uuid "7bdde4d8-daa6-55ef-bcc6-21f99b29d77c" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240905 1074 "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $g(-3)=-25$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{-}}({x}^{2}-34)=(-3)^{2}-34=-25, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{+}}({x}^{3}+2)=(-3)^{3}+2=-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)=-25.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)=-25,$ and the value of the function is $g(-3)=-25.$ So, statement II is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)=g(-3)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, the function is continuous at $x=-3,$ and statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240906 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240415 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240906 1069 "q-104577" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240906 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240906 1071 "Which of the following functions has a jump discontinuity at $x=-3?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240906 1072 #uuid "105aed96-f5f2-5f1f-857e-51a24598c677" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240906 1074 "The function $y=g(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $g(-3)=-2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}g(x)=-2,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-3$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\n![](images/38/3811fcaaf0bbf2158a046dca5c8dc232fd268bce664c810df9fe114a404296cf.png)\n\nNotice that $h(x)$ and $p(x)$ are continuous at $x=-3,$ while $f(x)$ and $q(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=-3,$ but they are *not* jump discontinuities." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240907 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240603 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240907 1069 "q-104582" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240907 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240907 1071 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative in their respective domains?\n\n- $F=(2x-4y)\\,i+(4x-8y)\\,j$\n- $G={2}^{2x+y+1}\\,i+{2}^{2x+y}\\,j$\n- $H=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\,i+\\frac{2{x}^{2}y}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240907 1072 #uuid "aac0da30-e1e4-5293-838c-ddb161ffe326" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240907 1074 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.\n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=2x-4y$ and $Q=4x-8y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-4,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P={2}^{2x+y+1}$ and $Q={2}^{2x+y}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={2}^{2x+y+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={2}^{2x+y+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)$ and $Q=\\frac{2{x}^{2}y}{{y}^{2}+1}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{4xy}{{y}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{4xy}{{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240908 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240568 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240908 1069 "q-104583" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240908 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240908 1071 "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $f(t)=4{t}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by $g(t)=6t.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240908 1072 #uuid "cd910782-fb38-585e-aeb6-b8d46e91a89b" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240908 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)=\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{t}^{2}\\cdot 6t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}24{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[6{t}^{4}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6-0 \\\\  & =6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}36{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[12{t}^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =12-0 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{12}\\cdot 6t \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240909 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240382 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240909 1069 "q-104586" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240909 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240909 1071 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $2$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $p(t)=5{t}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by $q(t)=t+1.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240909 1072 #uuid "4d406e5c-2737-5c6c-bad0-bb58ddb067d2" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240909 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{q(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)=\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(5{t}^{2}){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{-1}+(5{t}^{2}){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{0}+(5{t}^{2}){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0+0+10 \\\\  & =10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),q(t)⟩ & =q(-1)q(-1)+q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{-1}+(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{0}+(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0+1+4 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5}\\cdot (t+1) \\\\  & =2(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240910 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240435 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240910 1069 "q-104587" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240910 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240910 1071 "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $f(t)=12({t}^{2}-t)$ onto the subspace spanned by $g(t)=t-1.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240910 1072 #uuid "99c91096-e517-53ed-91eb-c1bbb59a1a8a" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240910 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)=\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}12({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (t-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(12{t}^{3}-24{t}^{2}+12t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[3{t}^{4}-8{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(3-8+6)-0 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-2t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-1+1)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =3(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240911 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240460 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240911 1069 "q-104617" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240911 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240911 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2x$ for  $x\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240911 1072 #uuid "7b025c1d-a393-5ea5-8c4e-6e0786f6f25d" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240911 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2\\pi )-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =(0+1)-(0+1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240912 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240776 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240912 1069 "q-104623" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240912 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240912 1071 "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y-{y}^{2}x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240912 1072 #uuid "1eb7495c-4bb1-5f06-bc1d-e458481de613" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240912 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}\\cdot 2-{2}^{2}\\cdot \\pi )-({0}^{2}\\cdot 0-{0}^{2}\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =(2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240913 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240350 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240913 1069 "q-104625" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240913 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240913 1071 "Let $f(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}).$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{3}+1$ for $x\\in [1,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240913 1072 #uuid "45e3e133-a99c-58ca-b74f-4cbfe235bb72" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240913 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{3}+1⟩,\\,t\\in [1,2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨1,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,2)$ and $(2,9).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f(2,9)-f(1,2) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2})-{e}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot 1-{e}^{1}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240914 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240608 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240914 1069 "q-104626" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240914 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240914 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}).$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [-1,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240914 1072 #uuid "893763e7-56bb-5bf7-9f2b-99426982714b" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240914 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in [-1,2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨-1,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(-1,2)$ and $(2,8).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(-1)) \\\\  & =f(2,8)-f(-1,2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3}+{8}^{3})-\\frac{1}{3}((-1)^{3}+{2}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(520)-\\frac{1}{3}(7) \\\\  & =\\frac{520}{3}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =171.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240915 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240419 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240915 1069 "q-104640" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240915 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240915 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240915 1072 #uuid "377033ea-e89e-5b49-a8b3-206ce2c51bb2" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240915 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(2\\pi ) & =⟨1,0,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0,0)$ and $(1,0,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,0,2\\pi )-f(1,0,0) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}\\cdot 2\\pi +1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0)-({1}^{2}\\cdot 0+1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =(2\\pi +0)-0 \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240916 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240554 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240916 1069 "q-104682" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240916 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240916 1071 "![](images/87/87c27d8114a9d314fdb93d1722d700a41cdbf82d0af27df58f0188ff67daca05.png)\n\nFor which points does the function $y=f(x)$ above have a jump discontinuity?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240916 1072 #uuid "b98ff455-03fc-5462-bdcf-14ff66832ec2" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240916 1074 "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-1)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to ,(-1)^{+}}f(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nIn the same way, the function has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)=-1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=4,$ but this is not a jump discontinuity." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240917 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240754 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240917 1069 "q-104689" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240917 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240917 1071 "![](images/2c/2cb9e5fc9fb0e5b1ea95406ba9961cc176a9c637f354b1e8ff2d574cbb1261d6.png)\n\nFor which points does the function $y=f(x)$ above have a jump discontinuity?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240917 1072 #uuid "5608c5f4-4191-57f4-a0f4-9aafd8b9e257" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240917 1074 "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)=4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=4,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but it is not a jump discontinuity.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $x=2$ only." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240918 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240764 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240918 1069 "q-104694" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240918 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240918 1071 "Which of the numbers below belong to ${Q}^{-}?$\n\n- $47$\n- $-\\sqrt{12}$\n- $\\sqrt{10}$\n- $0$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240918 1072 #uuid "016b340f-7aa5-5001-aeb3-927982c5cf50" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240918 1074 "Remember that ${Q}^{-}$ is the set of all negative rational numbers.\n\n- $47$ is positive, not negative, so it does *not* belong to ${Q}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $-\\sqrt{12}=-2\\sqrt{3}$ is not a rational number, so it does *not* belong to ${Q}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $\\sqrt{10}$ is positive, not negative, so it does *not* belong to ${Q}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $0$ is not negative, so it does *not* belong to ${Q}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"None of those listed\"." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240919 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240627 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240919 1069 "q-10470" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240919 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240919 1071 "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{25}-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}=1.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240919 1072 #uuid "d4a55ef3-3ecb-5cd8-a614-da45afdd2526" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240919 1074 "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na=\\sqrt{25}=5,\\,b=\\sqrt{9}=3,\\,h=2,\\,k=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\ny-2=\\pm \\frac{5}{3}(x-2).\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240920 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240620 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240920 1069 "q-104700" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240920 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240920 1071 "Which of the numbers below belong to  ${R}_{0}^{-}?$\n\n- $-14$\n- $\\sqrt{19}$\n- $-\\frac{2}{7}$\n- $0$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240920 1072 #uuid "c84fa181-6eb1-5e84-b542-36ff40a06a4c" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240920 1074 "Remember that ${R}_{0}^{-}$ is the set of all negative real numbers and $0.$\n\n- $-14$ belongs to ${R}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\n- $\\sqrt{19}$ is a positive number, so it does *not* belong to ${R}_{0}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $-\\frac{2}{7}$ belongs to ${R}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\n- $0$ belongs to ${R}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240921 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240565 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240921 1069 "q-104702" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240921 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240921 1071 "Which of the numbers below belong to  ${Z}_{0}^{-}?$\n\n- $-62$\n- $0$\n- $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n- $-\\frac{56}{4}$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240921 1072 #uuid "0e62d29f-2834-5b4e-b720-bc361e2a5dc0" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240921 1074 "Remember that ${Z}_{0}^{-}$ is the set of all negative integer numbers and $0.$\n\n- $-62$ belongs to ${Z}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\n- $0$ belongs to ${Z}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\n- $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is not an integer, so it does *not* belong to ${Z}_{0}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $-\\frac{56}{4}=-14$ belongs to ${Z}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only\"." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240922 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240656 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240922 1069 "q-104703" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240922 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240922 1071 "To which of the following sets does the number $\\sqrt{121}$ belong?\n\n- $N$\n- ${Q}^{-}$\n- ${Z}^{-}$\n- $Z$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240922 1072 #uuid "1da25f7d-182b-5d2d-b5c8-9d3f66bb445c" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240922 1074 "First, we note that $\\sqrt{121}=11$ is a natural number, so it belongs to $N.$\n\nSince every natural number is also an integer number, $\\sqrt{121}$ also belongs to $Z.$ \n\nOn the other hand, the sets ${Z}^{-}$ and ${Q}^{-}$ only contain negative numbers. Therefore, $\\sqrt{121}$ does not belong to either of these sets. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240923 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240411 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240923 1069 "q-10472" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240923 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240923 1071 "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9}=1.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240923 1072 #uuid "68b87203-b69d-5591-9e74-0feb6e886698" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240923 1074 "The standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(0,-2),\\,a=\\sqrt{16}=4,\\,b=\\sqrt{9}=3.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\ny+2=\\pm \\frac{3}{4}x.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240924 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240783 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240924 1069 "q-104725" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240924 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240924 1071 "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{e}^{t}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=(3{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2x)\\,i+(3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4y)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240924 1072 #uuid "227ca00b-2249-5c8a-8773-a9971e9ef701" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240924 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =3{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,e⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(1,e).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,e)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =({1}^{3}\\cdot {e}^{3}+{1}^{2}-2\\cdot {e}^{2})-({0}^{3}\\cdot {1}^{3}+{0}^{2}-2\\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{3}+1-2{e}^{2}+2 \\\\  & ={e}^{3}-2{e}^{2}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240925 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240468 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240925 1069 "q-104728" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240925 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240925 1071 "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+1,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240925 1072 #uuid "30f2f02c-3a13-5617-8d20-05ffc71d40c0" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240925 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r(2\\pi ) & =⟨4{\\pi }^{2}+1,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $(4{\\pi }^{2}+1,2\\pi ).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(4{\\pi }^{2}+1,2\\pi )-f(1,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}+1}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi -{e}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & ={e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}+1}-e \\\\  & =e({e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240926 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240742 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240926 1069 "q-104731" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240926 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240926 1071 "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2t,{t}^{2}-1⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=(2xy-y)\\,i+({x}^{2}-x)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240926 1072 #uuid "e735c8c7-7a76-5786-82b2-e7e846ae7fe8" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240926 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2xy-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}y-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-1⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-1)$ and $(4,3).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(4,3)-f(0,-1) \\\\  & =({4}^{2}\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 3)-({0}^{2}\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =48-12 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240927 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240533 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240927 1069 "q-104733" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240927 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240927 1071 "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,{t}^{2}⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=({x}^{2}-y)\\,i+({y}^{2}-x)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240927 1072 #uuid "7794dd99-ebef-5626-aacd-8b9fce4aa6b2" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240927 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot 4)-(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3}-0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =9{ln}^{3}⁡2+\\frac{64}{3}-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =9{ln}^{3}⁡2-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240928 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240636 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240928 1069 "q-104734" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240928 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240928 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)={t}^{2}+t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}-t,\\,{p}_{3}(t)={t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $2$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240928 1072 #uuid "95a9cd65-803f-5d19-875e-716631b2f8a2" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240928 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}+t,{t}^{2}-t⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{-1}+({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =((-1)^{2}\\,+\\,(-1))\\,\\cdot \\,((-1)^{2}\\,-\\,(-1))\\,+\\,({0}^{2}\\,+\\,0)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,0)\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,+\\,1)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}+t,{t}^{2}-1⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,1){|}_{-1}\\,+\\,({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,1){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =((-1)^{2}\\,+\\,(-1))\\,\\cdot \\,((-1)^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,+\\,({0}^{2}\\,+\\,0)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,+\\,1)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Let's check if ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}-t,{t}^{2}-1⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,1){|}_{-1}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,1){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =((-1)^{2}\\,-\\,(-1))\\,\\cdot \\,((-1)^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,+\\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,0)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, $S$ is an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240929 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240576 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240929 1069 "q-104736" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240929 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240929 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)={t}^{2}-4,\\,{p}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}-t,\\,{p}_{3}(t)={t}^{2}-2t.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $2$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)+p(2)q(2),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240929 1072 #uuid "5d2cd958-2383-51a8-8ef7-f3c1ff9a5b43" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240929 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}-4,{t}^{2}-t⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{2}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,0)\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,+\\,({2}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({2}^{2}\\,-\\,2(2)) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}-t,{t}^{2}-2t⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{1}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{2}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\,-\\,0)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,2(0))\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,2(1))\\,+\\,({2}^{2}\\,-\\,2)\\,\\cdot \\,({2}^{2}-2(2)) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}-4,{t}^{2}-2t⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{1}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{2}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,2(0))\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,2(1))\\,+\\,({2}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({2}^{2}\\,-\\,2(2)) \\\\  & =3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240501 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240930 1069 "q-104739" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240930 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240930 1071 "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y})\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y={x}^{2}+{sin}^{2}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240930 1072 #uuid "86f7c787-e5df-5794-8bba-c906aa91522f" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240930 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y})}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}+{sin}^{2}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}})-({0}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =(0+{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}})-(0+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{{\\pi }^{2}}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240539 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240931 1069 "q-104745" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240931 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240931 1071 "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=({e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,i+(x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y=x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240931 1072 #uuid "7507d02e-e1da-50a2-9a9d-6236d6a7afae" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240931 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{({e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{(x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,\\pi -1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(\\pi ,\\pi -1).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,\\pi -1)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(\\pi \\cdot {e}^{\\pi -1}+(\\pi -1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )-(0\\cdot {e}^{1}+1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =(\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}+0)-0 \\\\  & =\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240932 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240557 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240932 1069 "q-104747" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240932 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240932 1071 "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240932 1072 #uuid "b5c03f65-0c80-5089-93df-00e7a5829280" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240932 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,2⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},e⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},e).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},e)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡e)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡e-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240933 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240406 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240933 1069 "q-10476" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240933 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240933 1071 "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $4{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}-32x+18y+19=0.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240933 1072 #uuid "99413b53-4629-51ac-943e-07c35ae3c4e3" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240933 1074 "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows