ion $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2x{e}^{-y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2x{e}^{-y}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{-y}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)⟩,\\,t\\in [2,3].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨2,0⟩, \\\\ r(3) & =⟨3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(2,0)$ and $(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-f(2,0) \\\\  & =({3}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2})-({2}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-0}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2-4 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240935 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240464 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240935 1069 "q-104764" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240935 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240935 1071 "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=(6{x}^{2}-y)\\,i-x\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y={x}^{2}-{e}^{\\sqrt{x+1}}$ for $x\\in [0,3].$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240935 1072 #uuid "436ac2b7-bdc3-58c8-9890-0935ce7a366c" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240935 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{(6{x}^{2}-y)}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{(-x)}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =6{x}^{2}-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2{x}^{3}-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}-{e}^{\\sqrt{t+1}}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,3].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-e⟩, \\\\ r(3) & =⟨3,9-{e}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-e)$ and $(3,9-{e}^{2}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3,9-{e}^{2})-f(0,e) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {3}^{3}-3\\cdot (9-{e}^{2}))-(2\\cdot {0}^{3}-0\\cdot (-e)) \\\\  & =(54-27+3{e}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =(27+3{e}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =3({e}^{2}+9).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240936 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240349 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240936 1069 "q-104769" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240936 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240936 1071 "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you're given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(4)=-2,\\,{f}^{′}(4)=4,\\,g(4)=1,\\,{g}^{′}(4)=0\n$$\n\nIf $h(x)=\\frac{2-3f(x)}{g(x)+1},$ compute ${h}^{′}(4).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240936 1072 #uuid "8949d829-346e-5e9a-a086-bcaa4c506f76" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240936 1074 "The quotient rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{u}{v})=\\frac{{u}^{′}v-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2-3f(x)$ and $v=g(x)+1.$ Differentiating each, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the quotient rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}v-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot (g(x)+1)-(2-3f(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{{(g(x)+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=4,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{-3{f}^{′}(4)\\cdot (g(4)+1)-(2-3f(4))\\cdot {g}^{′}(4)}{{(g(4)+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\cdot 4\\cdot (1+1)-(2-3\\cdot (-2))\\cdot 0}{{(1+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240675 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240937 1069 "q-104778" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240937 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240937 1071 "Which of the following functions has the codomain ${0,1}?$\n\n- $p:{0,1}\\to Z$\n- $q:[0,1]\\to {0,1}$\n- $r:{0,1}\\to (0,1)$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240937 1072 #uuid "1f571336-e197-53b2-942f-7691710c5fd2" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240937 1074 "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The codomain of $p$ is $Z,$ the set of integers.\n\n- The codomain of $q$ is ${0,1},$ the set containing the two numbers $0$ and $1$ only.\n\n- The codomain of $r$ is $(0,1),$ the interval from $0$ to $1$ (excluding both $0$ and $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240938 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240380 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240938 1069 "q-104780" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240938 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240938 1071 "Which of the following functions has the domain $Z?$\n\n- $p:Z\\to [0,3]$\n- $q:(0,7)\\to Z$\n- $r:{Z}^{+}\\to Z$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240938 1072 #uuid "00f5a8c6-457a-5b26-87c0-0c588245cf9d" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240938 1074 "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The domain of $p$ is $Z,$ the set of integers.\n\n- The domain of $q$ is $(0,7),$ the interval from $0$ to $7$ (excluding $0$ and $7$).\n\n- The domain of $r$ is ${Z}^{+},$ the set of positive integers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240939 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240445 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240939 1069 "q-104781" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240939 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240939 1071 "Which of the following functions has the domain $[-2,2]?$\n\n- $p:[-2,2]\\to (-2,2)$\n- $r:[-2,2]\\to {-2,2}$\n- $q:Z\\to [-2,2]$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240939 1072 #uuid "f3958191-a06f-54c9-9b69-6e7038fa786f" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240939 1074 "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The domain of $p$ is $[-2,2],$ the interval from $-2$ to $2$ (including $-2$ and $2$).\n\n- The domain of $q$ is $[-2,2],$ the interval from $-2$ to $2$ (including $-2$ and $2$).\n\n- The domain of $r$ is $Z,$ the set of integers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240940 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240438 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240940 1069 "q-104782" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240940 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240940 1071 "![](images/2a/2a11d5b84bdf66b6406f9465ba6a8bc8ecdf364bea2dcd6902034990a74ef0a9.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(-3).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240940 1072 #uuid "66a00b85-1c8b-5d71-a8f5-a30ecfdeb00b" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240940 1074 "To compute ${f}^{′}(-3),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=-3.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ on $[-5,-2].$ Notice that $x=-3$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](images/59/59159512fa1b51477f171584a3717e2bf2774e8c940b59525fe478e1bcf6aece.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (-5,-2)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{1-4}{-2-(-5)}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-3)=-1.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240941 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240360 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240941 1069 "q-104784" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240941 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240941 1071 "![](images/e5/e5bcb5723747beb905cc93e3bcf8e3a5be7bf566cf408998d3173490bd2486b5.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-3)$ where $h(x)=-3f(x)-g(x).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240941 1072 #uuid "a5f39790-6626-50e4-936f-eda5c3ab471c" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240941 1074 "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=-3{f}^{′}(x)-{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-3)=-3{f}^{′}(-3)-{g}^{′}(-3).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=-3$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-5,-2)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-5,-1)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/4f/4f8b5f4c3a87f6cb1c346a4672df96a068d74690f0b7c9c6f9a77a9a3710a34a.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-5,-2)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{-1-3}{-2-(-5)}=-\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-3)=-\\frac{4}{3}.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-5,-1)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{4-4}{-1-(-5)}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(-3)=0.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-3) & =-3{f}^{′}(-3)-{g}^{′}(-3) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3})-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240942 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240371 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240942 1069 "q-104785" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240942 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240942 1071 "![](images/df/df4fed951351dfc1203ba6257f404a5efadbec37b9f11098af1fc36a7793b0c7.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(2)$ where $h(x)=g(x)-f(x).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240942 1072 #uuid "f6c1e529-59cb-55e7-b759-671e209b110e" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240942 1074 "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x)-{f}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(2)={g}^{′}(2)-{f}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=2$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-1,3)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-1,3)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/81/814ce0fdce1428b3bd619caa5e8d09a828d2f2c52fd0b20b7f520562319fb848.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-1,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{4-1}{3-(-1)}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-1,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{1-4}{3-(-1)}=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(2)=-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & ={g}^{′}(2)-{f}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240943 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240399 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240943 1069 "q-104786" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240943 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240943 1071 "![](images/7c/7c4260d8cdf3557b6c6f762bf83862297812368d521104ba27995778a0cb0ff7.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-2)$ where $h(x)=f(x)+2g(x).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240943 1072 #uuid "5b935503-7332-565f-9b91-c43e7b0904d9" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240943 1074 "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)+2{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-2)={f}^{′}(-2)+2{g}^{′}(-2).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=-2$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-4,0)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-4,0)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/4c/4ced2c562500bfa0d352d4552f38f47b5caab9bdc68db5bc3dbfca43799a0f8f.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-4,0)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{0-4}{0-(-4)}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-4,0)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{2-4}{0-(-4)}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(-2)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-2) & ={f}^{′}(-2)+2{g}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & =-1+2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240944 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240579 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240944 1069 "q-104787" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240944 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240944 1071 "![](images/e0/e02f873d2d583767e0b29e7ad57121e23e5c73716ce058ef6bed09b644b37bce.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-1)$ where $h(x)=2f(x)+g(x).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240944 1072 #uuid "d460086c-0f96-558c-85a2-2debf9c57ea0" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240944 1074 "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=2{f}^{′}(x)+{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-1)=2{f}^{′}(-1)+{g}^{′}(-1).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=-1$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-4,0)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-2,1)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/f6/f64bb3d174950f3c4ac26d7d65d0b3b7881761fa3fb2ddd166f3a23c4af7fa27.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-4,0)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{0-4}{0-(-4)}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-2,1)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{2-4}{1-(-2)}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(-1)=-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-1) & =2{f}^{′}(-1)+{g}^{′}(-1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240945 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240673 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240945 1069 "q-104794" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240945 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240945 1071 "![](images/38/384ffa76d7b3d0aeb0e537122f3211eed5a54ff5a89fb65da8eba495d93a5b2f.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-1)$ where $h(x)=2f(x)-g(x).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240945 1072 #uuid "3b4cc581-b557-5cc0-9783-09b6acd6b3b4" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240945 1074 "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=2{f}^{′}(x)-{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-1)=2{f}^{′}(-1)-{g}^{′}(-1).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=-1$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-4,2)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-4,4)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/c5/c5c7836ea7cc3815ce86c90e05b73437c7d084d56527592cccdd626ab0e58367.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-4,2)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{4-4}{2-(-4)}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)=0.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-4,4)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{0-4}{4-(-4)}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(-1)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-1) & =2{f}^{′}(-1)-{g}^{′}(-1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (0)-(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240946 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240358 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240946 1069 "q-104795" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240946 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240946 1071 "![](images/61/61c9cb5fed816e028cf47e3d6b264a40a2fc4c04bc5d330e8662a8dfd8fa26e1.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(2)$ where $h(x)=f(x)g(x).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240946 1072 #uuid "fefd43d0-bf25-5b03-a050-63e64e251f1e" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240946 1074 "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(2)={f}^{′}(2)\\cdot g(2)+f(2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(2)=4$ and $g(2)=3.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-2,4)$ equals $0.$ So, ${f}^{′}(2)=0.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,4)$ equals $\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & ={f}^{′}(2)\\cdot g(2)+f(2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 3+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240947 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240356 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240947 1069 "q-10480" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240947 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240947 1071 "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $4{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}-48y-10x+59=0.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240947 1072 #uuid "f6c40104-e639-5d64-b783-bd0274fb3c73" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240947 1074 "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}-48y-10x+59 & =0 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}-48y-5{x}^{2}-10x+59 & =0 \\\\ 4[{y}^{2}-12y]-5[{x}^{2}+2x]+59 & =0 \\\\ 4[(y-6)^{2}-36]-5[(x+1)^{2}-1]+59 & =0 \\\\ 4(y-6)^{2}-144-5(x+1)^{2}+5+59 & =0 \\\\ 4(y-6)^{2}-5(x+1)^{2} & =144-5-59 \\\\ 4(y-6)^{2}-5(x+1)^{2} & =80 \\\\ \\frac{(y-6)^{2}}{20}-\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(-1,6),\\,a=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5},\\,b=\\sqrt{16}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\ny-6=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}(x+1).\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240948 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240508 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240948 1069 "q-104803" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240948 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240948 1071 "![](images/ec/ec9faab92da8de318d78e548adc35e8df20083816f8bafdacf0cfbbd497e084c.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-2)$ where $h(x)=f(x)g(x).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240948 1072 #uuid "720b1d63-4a80-5bc1-95a2-9593bdc22709" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240948 1074 "The product rule and the addition rule for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-2)={f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)+f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-2)=0$ and $g(-2)=3.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,0)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,0)$ equals $\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-2) & ={f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)+f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2)-{f}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 3+0\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240949 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240556 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240949 1069 "q-104813" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240949 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240949 1071 "![](images/93/931802565a13dac284496f41ebb19238402056689a96288b2fbb1dd9f018ae12.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(-1)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240949 1072 #uuid "9883ee29-6088-5901-9664-a2276e734a99" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240949 1074 "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(-1)\\cdot g(-1)-f(-1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-1)}{(g(-1))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(-1)=0$ and $g(-1)=1.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,2)$ equals $\\frac{2}{3}.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,2)$ equals $1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-1)=1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(-1)\\cdot g(-1)-f(-1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-1)}{(g(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240950 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240735 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240950 1069 "q-104827" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240950 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240950 1071 "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n*\"The function $g$ maps the set $X$ to the set containing the two numbers $-1$ and $1$ only.\"*" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240950 1072 #uuid "c9801a5b-a125-565d-921d-eaabd2179406" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240950 1074 "The sentence *\"The function $g$ maps the set $X$ to the set containing the two numbers $-1$ and $1$ only\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\,$ $g:X\\to {-1,1}$\n\nHere, the domain of $g$ is $X,$ and the codomain of $g$ is ${-1,1}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240951 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240761 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240951 1069 "q-10484" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240951 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240951 1071 "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $25{y}^{2}-9{x}^{2}=225.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240951 1072 #uuid "be689818-78df-5d0a-8d4e-d472b73371da" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240951 1074 "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{y}^{2}-9{x}^{2} & =225 \\\\ 25{y}^{2}-9(0)^{2} & =225 \\\\ 25{y}^{2} & =225 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ y & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $(0,\\pm 3).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240952 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240626 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240952 1069 "q-104845" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240952 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240952 1071 "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n*\"The function $g$ maps the set of integers to the set of rational numbers.\"*" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240952 1072 #uuid "5a267466-27f9-5907-b5d3-8a64993c6282" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240952 1074 "The sentence *\"The function $g$ maps the set of integers to the set of rational numbers\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\,$ $g:Z\\to Q$\n\nHere, the domain of $g$ is $Z,$ and the codomain of $g$ is $Q.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240953 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240434 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240953 1069 "q-104861" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240953 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240953 1071 "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b,$ given by\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U},\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $⟨{x}_{1},{x}_{2}⟩$ and $⟨{y}_{1},{y}_{2}⟩$ are the solutions of the systems $Ax=b$ and $Ly=b$ respectively, what is the value of the product ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{2}?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240953 1072 #uuid "90798e55-963c-5ed8-a201-d8aa9b66ebff" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240953 1074 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=1 \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=1$ and ${y}_{2}=4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=4$ and ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 4=-6.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240954 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240397 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240954 1069 "q-104862" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240954 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240954 1071 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $p(t)=2{t}^{2}-2t$ onto the subspace spanned by $q(t)=2t.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240954 1072 #uuid "7e5894fa-913f-5f99-9960-b72e3b7e0937" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240954 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{q(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)=\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,(2t){|}_{-1}+(2{t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,(2t){|}_{0}+(2{t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,(2t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-8+0+0 \\\\  & =-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),q(t)⟩ & =q(-1)q(-1)+q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(2t){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,(2t){|}_{-1}+(2t){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,(2t){|}_{0}+(2t){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,(2t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4+0+4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{8}\\cdot (2t) \\\\  & =-2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240955 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240498 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240955 1069 "q-104865" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240955 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240955 1071 "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $f(t)=16{t}^{3}+2$ onto the subspace spanned by $g(t)=3{t}^{2}-1.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240955 1072 #uuid "5af44cf5-05c6-5ddb-af78-b148bd717116" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240955 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)=\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{3}+2)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(48{t}^{5}-16{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[8{t}^{6}-4{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}-2t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(8-4+2-2)-0 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{t}^{2}-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{4}-6{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\frac{9{t}^{5}}{5}-2{t}^{3}+t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{5}-2+1)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(\\frac{4}{5})}\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =5(3{t}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240956 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240564 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240956 1069 "q-104871" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240956 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240956 1071 "Let $V={R}_{1}[t],$ the vector space of real polynomials of degree at most $1,$ and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1).\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t-1,\\,q(t)=4t-3\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t)}$ equals $t.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240956 1072 #uuid "e18df242-9d5f-58ab-883f-e2f596589751" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240956 1074 "Since the set ${{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)=t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t)=\\frac{⟨q(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{p}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =q(0){p}_{2}(0)+q(1){p}_{2}(1) \\\\  & =(4t-3){|}_{0}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{0}+(4t-3){|}_{1}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={p}_{2}(0){p}_{2}(0)+{p}_{2}(1){p}_{2}(1) \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}{|}_{0}+(t-1)^{2}{|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) & =\\frac{3}{1}\\cdot (t-1)=3t-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =t+(3t-3) \\\\  & =4t-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240957 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240777 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240957 1069 "q-104877" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240957 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240957 1071 "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $(-4,1)?$ You may assume that all points of discontinuity for each function are shown." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240957 1072 #uuid "114510d5-65f3-59a1-8667-fed3c1e774c1" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240957 1074 "The correct answer is the following. \n\n![](images/44/441277a2f5c960a02d84707d21746da558e136168dc951a931f5354abf4a283e.png)\n\nIndeed, we have that $g(x)$ is continuous on the open interval $(-4,1).$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-4,1),$\n\n- $p(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-3,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-4,1),$\n\n- $h(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-2,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-4,1),$\n\n- $q(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-1,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-4,1).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240958 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240598 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240958 1069 "q-104881" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240958 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240958 1071 "![](images/d6/d6efb9c0bc8d7114c23ad3276303fe6898afcc4e27ad32db354f8e939d6f1c2e.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous? You may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240958 1072 #uuid "6b3df942-5e3a-5677-95e3-1406da19ede2" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240958 1074 "To determine the intervals of continuity of a function, we take the domain of the function and exclude any points of discontinuity.\n\nThe function is defined on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and $(-3,\\infty ).$ There is a discontinuity at $x=-3,$ but this is already excluded. \n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=4,$ so we need to exclude that.\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-3,4),$ and $(4,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $(-3,1)$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- $(-\\infty ,1)$ and $(-5,0)$ contain the excluded point $x=-3,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals.\n\n- $(1,5)$ and $(0,\\infty )$ contain the point of discontinuity $x=4$, so the function is not continuous on these intervals." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240555 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240959 1069 "q-104882" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240959 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240959 1071 "![](images/d0/d068feef885b3bb1ca3770a2d2648e9953f3ca9e1823111c507934b39ad71a76.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous? You may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240959 1072 #uuid "5af15140-a76f-5129-96a3-2cad91305c74" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240959 1074 "To determine the intervals of continuity of a function, we take the domain of the function and exclude any points of discontinuity.\n\nThe function is defined on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(-1,\\infty ).$ There is a discontinuity at $x=-1,$ but this is already excluded.\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(-1,\\infty ).$  It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $(-5,-1)$ \n\nNotice that the intervals $(-3,\\infty ),$  $(-3,5),$  $(-\\infty ,0)$  and $(-3,10)$ contain the excluded point $x=-1,$ so the function is not continuous on any of these  these intervals." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240960 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240597 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240960 1069 "q-104887" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240960 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240960 1071 "If $X={{3}^{2},{5}^{2},{7}^{2},{9}^{2}}$, then $|X|=$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240960 1072 #uuid "0119f471-8ba7-5379-8213-1f9d97c1ac27" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240960 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $X$ is the number of elements in $X.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $X:$\n\n$$\n{3}^{2},\\,{5}^{2},\\,{7}^{2},\\,{9}^{2}\n$$\n\nSince $X$ has $4$ elements, its cardinality is $|X|=4.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240961 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240361 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240961 1069 "q-104894" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240961 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240961 1071 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of real polynomials of degree at most $2,$ and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1).\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}-1,\\,q(t)={t}^{2}+t\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{2}(t)}$ equals $0.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240961 1072 #uuid "e0a44c8c-788c-548d-8b09-deb0ad2858dc" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240961 1074 "Since the set ${{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t)=0,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)=\\frac{⟨q(t),{p}_{1}(t)⟩}{⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{1}(t)⟩}\\,{p}_{1}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),{p}_{1}(t)⟩ & =q(-1){p}_{1}(-1)+q(0){p}_{1}(0)+q(1){p}_{1}(1) \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{-1}\\,\\,\\cdot \\,(t){|}_{-1}+({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{0}\\,\\,\\cdot \\,(t){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{1}\\,\\,\\cdot \\,(t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{1}(t)⟩ & ={p}_{1}(-1){p}_{1}(-1)+{p}_{1}(0){p}_{1}(0)+{p}_{1}(1){p}_{1}(1) \\\\  & =(t)^{2}{|}_{-1}+(t)^{2}{|}_{0}+(t)^{2}{|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t) & =\\frac{2}{2}\\cdot t=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =t+0 \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240962 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240607 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240962 1069 "q-104909" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240962 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240962 1071 "If $A={1,{10,12},15,{22,26,28},{42,62}}$, then $|A|=$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240962 1072 #uuid "461f9697-850b-5c06-91d1-f6409fa7f2d1" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240962 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $A:$\n\n$$\n1,\\,{10,12},\\,15,\\,{22,26,28},\\,{42,62}\n$$\n\nNote that ${10,12},$ ${22,26,28},$ and ${42,62}$ each count as single elements of $A.$\n\nSince $A$ has $5$ elements, the cardinality is $|A|=5.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240963 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240642 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240963 1069 "q-104916" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240963 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240963 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A={{12},{20,22},{6,9,15}}?$\n\n- ${-1,{12}}$\n- ${{20,22}}$\n- ${{0},{6,9,15}}$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240963 1072 #uuid "4740436b-fb84-571a-aea1-e76558938555" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240963 1074 "A set is a subset of $A$ if all its elements are elements of $A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each option.\n\n- ${-1,{12}}$ is *not* a subset of $A$ because it contains the element $-1\\notin A.\\,\\times $\n\n- ${{20,22}}$ is a subset of $A.\\,✓$\n\n- ${{0},{6,9,15}}$ is *not* a subset of $A$ because it contains the element ${0}\\notin A.\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240519 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240964 1069 "q-10492" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240964 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240964 1071 "Calculate the $x$-coordinates of the points where the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}=1$\nintersects the line $y=x.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240964 1072 #uuid "29c67250-cd7a-5993-b767-a56208edceaf" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240964 1074 "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=x,$ we first replace $y$ with $x$ in the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5{x}^{2}}{20}-\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{20} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{20} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =20 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240965 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240370 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240965 1069 "q-104924" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240965 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240965 1071 "Find all of the subsets of $S={r,t}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240965 1072 #uuid "0dd42d85-d6a7-55c7-82e3-571dbcce8a0f" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240965 1074 "To find every subset of $S,$ we list out every combination of elements within $S.$\n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](images/02/022399e6a7a5e1f79b58e856aa8e9e5e9f3561e8e4bcb5b80a95237d30d06002.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,{r},{t},{r,t}.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240966 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240440 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240966 1069 "q-104925" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240966 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240966 1071 "Find all of the subsets of $A={0,1}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240966 1072 #uuid "e86b2fe1-a7b6-5974-a665-b72e71d6506e" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240966 1074 "To find every subset of $A,$ we list out every combination of elements within $A.$ \n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](images/bb/bb31e2bd632f39706b2785ce6cab764673997fe77b8a9417f7dc213cdff4b7a7.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set, since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,{0},{1},{0,1}.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240967 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240622 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240967 1069 "q-104927" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240967 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240967 1071 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $2$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $p(t)={t}^{2}+3t-2$ onto the subspace spanned by $q(t)=2t-2.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240967 1072 #uuid "9efe6587-6cb2-5872-b27b-b278f7934660" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240967 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{q(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)=\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1) \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,+\\,3t\\,-\\,2){|}_{t=-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\cdot \\,(2t\\,-\\,2){|}_{t=-1}\\,\\,\\,+\\,({t}^{2}\\,+\\,3t\\,-\\,2){|}_{t=0}\\,\\,\\cdot \\,(2t\\,-\\,2){|}_{t=0}\\,\\,\\,+\\,({t}^{2}\\,+\\,3t\\,-\\,2){|}_{t=1}\\,\\,\\,\\cdot (2t\\,-\\,2){|}_{t=1} \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot (-4)+(-2)\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =16+4 \\\\  & =20,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),q(t)⟩ & =q(-1)q(-1)+q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(2t\\,-\\,2){|}_{t=-1}\\,\\,\\,\\cdot \\,(2t\\,-\\,2){|}_{t=-1}\\,\\,\\,+(2t\\,-\\,2){|}_{t=0}\\,\\,\\,\\cdot \\,(2t\\,-\\,2){|}_{t=0}\\,\\,\\,+(2t\\,-\\,2){|}_{t=1}\\,\\,\\,\\cdot \\,(2t\\,-\\,2){|}_{t=1} \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot (-4)+(-2)\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =16+4+0 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{20}\\cdot (2t-2) \\\\  & =2t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240781 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240968 1069 "q-104936" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240968 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240968 1071 "Let $V=C[-1,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[-1,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $f(t)={t}^{3}+10{t}^{2}+2$ onto the subspace spanned by $g(t)=5{t}^{2}+1.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240968 1072 #uuid "6a0afbf2-8aa8-5003-9b37-01bec551a919" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240968 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)=\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({t}^{3}+10{t}^{2}+2)\\cdot (5{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(5{t}^{5}+50{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}+20{t}^{2}+2)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{5}{6}{t}^{6}+10{t}^{5}+\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{4}+\\frac{20}{3}{t}^{3}+2t]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{6}+10+\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{20}{3}+2)-(\\frac{5}{6}-10+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{20}{3}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{112}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(5{t}^{2}+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(25{t}^{4}+10{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[5{t}^{5}+\\frac{10}{3}{t}^{3}+t]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(5+\\frac{10}{3}+1)-(-5-\\frac{10}{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{112}{3})}{(\\frac{56}{3})}\\cdot (5{t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =2(5{t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =10{t}^{2}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240969 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240716 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240969 1069 "q-104938" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240969 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240969 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $f:x↦2x-{x}^{3}?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240969 1072 #uuid "9712ef8c-b0d4-5afe-81b9-8d8c04b7e6ba" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240969 1074 "The notation $f:x↦2x-{x}^{3}$ means that the function $f$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to $2x-{x}^{3}$ in the codomain. This can also be written as\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-{x}^{3}.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240970 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240387 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240970 1069 "q-104940" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240970 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240970 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $h:x↦{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240970 1072 #uuid "07541169-bcdc-5b49-881c-ed93b3125c57" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240970 1074 "The notation $h:x↦{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ means that the function $h$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to ${x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ in the codomain. This can also be written as\n\n$$\nh(x)={x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240971 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240459 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240971 1069 "q-104945" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240971 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240971 1071 "Which of the following is the correct interpretation of $g:{0,1}\\to Z?$\n\n- The domain of $g$ is ${0,1}$\n- The codomain of $g$ is $Z$\n- The range of $g$ is ${0,1}$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240971 1072 #uuid "caad9560-3559-5568-b085-de268d2408da" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240971 1074 "The notation \n\n$$\ng:{0,1}\\to Z\n$$\n\nmeans that the function $g$ maps the set ${0,1}$ to the set $Z.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $g$ is ${0,1},$ and\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $Z.$ Note that ${0,1}⊆Z$ is *not* necessarily the range.\n\nAs a result, statements I and II are true, while statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240972 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240388 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240972 1069 "q-104946" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240972 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240972 1071 "Which of the following is the correct interpretation of $g:Q\\to R?$\n\n- The domain of $g$ is $R$\n- The codomain of $g$ is $R$\n- The range of $g$ is $Q$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240972 1072 #uuid "ece458da-55a3-56bf-9d0d-49bed0ba9d3c" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240972 1074 "The notation \n\n$$\ng:Q\\to R\n$$\n\nmeans that the function $g$ maps the set $Q$ to the set $R.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $Q,$ and\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $R.$ Note that $Q⊂R$ is *not* necessarily the range.\n\nAs a result, statements I and III are false, while statement II is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240973 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240649 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240973 1069 "q-10495" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240973 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240973 1071 "Calculate the points where the hyperbola $9{y}^{2}-20{x}^{2}=180$ intersects the line $x=6.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240973 1072 #uuid "20fdb8ef-5a55-5862-8b1f-e7e0a7c2a336" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240973 1074 "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $x=6,$ we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{y}^{2}-20{x}^{2} & =180 \\\\ 9{y}^{2}-20(6)^{2} & =180 \\\\ 9{y}^{2}-720 & =180 \\\\ {y}^{2}-80 & =20 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =80+20 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =100 \\\\ y & =\\pm 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the hyperbola intersects the vertical line $x=6$  at the points $(6,\\pm 10).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240974 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240724 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240974 1069 "q-104954" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240974 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240974 1071 "![](images/8f/8ff01afa1993f8e301edb69e3e7a13ee71ab01b1a3da416aa53ee94d021184e1.png)\n\nConsider the function $g:X\\to Y$ given by the mapping diagram shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The domain of $g$ is ${0,1,2}$\n- The codomain of $g$ is ${-1,0,1,2}$\n- The range of $g$ is ${0,1,2}$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240974 1072 #uuid "86293bdd-1445-5c75-b0fe-3a52f74f9647" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240974 1074 "The notation $g:X\\to Y$ means that the function $g$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $Y.$\n\nThe range of $g$ is the subset of the codomain that contains all values $y\\in Y$ that are mapped to by at least one element $x\\in X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect our diagram:\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $X={-1,0,1,2},$\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $Y={0,1,2},$\n\n- the range of $g$ is ${0,1,2},$ the subset of elements of $Y$ that have at least one incoming arrow.\n\nAs a result, statements I and II are false, while statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer \"III only.\"" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240975 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240637 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240975 1069 "q-104984" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240975 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240975 1071 "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(2{x}^{2}-1) & 2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\ 2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} & {e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(2{y}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(0,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(0,0)>0$\n- $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)>0$\n- The point $(0,0)$ is a local maximum of $f$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240975 1072 #uuid "bcc06ba1-1065-5981-a107-14dc46cea80e" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240975 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=(-1)^{2}-{0}^{2}=1>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=-1<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(0,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)<0,$ the point $(0,0)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240976 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240357 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240976 1069 "q-104995" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240976 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240976 1071 "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3y & 3{y}^{2}-3x\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(1,1)>0$\n- $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)<0$\n- The point $(1,1)$ is a local maximum of $f$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240976 1072 #uuid "e273e9fd-fb16-5b0e-b15b-fcbb70918d78" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240976 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3y & 3{y}^{2}-3x\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}-3y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}-3x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-3y)=6x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-3x)=6y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-3x)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6x & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=det({f}^{″}(1,1))={6}^{2}-(-3)^{2}=27>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=6>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(1,1)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)>0,$ the point $(1,1)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240977 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240779 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240977 1069 "q-1050" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240977 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240977 1071 "What is the coefficient matrix for the following system of equations?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=7 \\\\ 2x=4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240977 1072 #uuid "9f4f0dcb-c27e-5a97-859f-1df6c594fb28" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240977 1074 "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered  $x,y$ \n on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=7 \\\\ 2x+0y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240978 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240762 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240978 1069 "q-105033" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240978 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240978 1071 "Which of the following are subsets of ${Q}_{0}^{-}$ (the set of non-positive rational numbers)?\n\n- $Z$\n- ${Z}^{-}$ (the set of negative integers)\n- $N$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240978 1072 #uuid "63db395b-88f4-58c0-8886-5830de0a85c3" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240978 1074 "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- $Z$ is *not* a subset of ${Q}_{0}^{-}.$ For example, $1\\in Z$ but $1\\notin {Q}_{0}^{-}.$\n\n- ${Z}^{-}$ is  a subset of ${Q}_{0}^{-}.$ Every negative integer is a non-positive rational number.\n\n- $N$ is *not* a subset of ${Q}_{0}^{-}.$ For example, $1\\in N$ but $1\\notin {Q}_{0}^{-}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240979 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240715 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240979 1069 "q-105034" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240979 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240979 1071 "Which of the following are subsets of $R?$\n\n- ${Q}^{-}$ (the set of negative rationals)\n- ${Z}^{+}$ (the set of positive integers)\n- $N$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240979 1072 #uuid "eb139d93-059f-53af-a8f9-6a747e8ff56b" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240979 1074 "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- ${Q}^{-}$ is  a subset of $R.$ Every negative rational number is a real number.\n\n- ${Z}^{+}$ is a subset of $R.$ Every positive integer number is a real number.\n\n- $N$ a subset of $R.$ Every natural number is a real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III\"." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240980 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240574 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240980 1069 "q-105046" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240980 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240980 1071 "![](images/0b/0bc0e75bb577ca37b89568e14a4e0d03da7ce4f4853c4e8c57d9b2df79897b13.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=0$\n- $∇\\cdot F>0$\n- $∇\\cdot F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240980 1072 #uuid "3b5e7ba9-0b08-5996-9edf-60b5549da2af" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240980 1074 "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere, the vector field $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240981 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240355 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240981 1069 "q-105064" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240981 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240981 1071 "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={n\\in N∣n-2\\ge 4}$ and $B={n\\in Z∣n+2\\ge 5}?$\n\n- $A⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $∅⊆A$\n- $B⊇A$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240981 1072 #uuid "c0ffa6ad-f9fd-59d8-a6a4-bd61246d63d4" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240981 1074 "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $n-2\\ge 4,$ that is to say, $n\\ge 6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA={6,7,8,9,10,11,…}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n+2\\ge 5,$ That is to say, $n\\ge 3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB={3,4,5,6,7,8,…}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is true. Every element in the set $A$ is an element of the set $B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is false. For example, $3\\in B$ but $3\\notin A.$\n\n- $∅⊆A$ is true. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\n- $B⊇A$ is equivalent to $A⊆B,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only.\"" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240982 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240490 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240982 1069 "q-105065" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240982 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240982 1071 "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in Z∣x\\le 5}$ and $B={x\\in Z∣-7\\le x<3}?$\n\n- $A⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $B⊈A$\n- $A⊇B$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240982 1072 #uuid "e6954872-228a-577f-9a57-37ed3b0eaeb9" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240982 1074 "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the integer numbers that satisfy the inequality $x\\le 5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA={…,-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $-7\\le x<3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB={-7,-6,-5,-4,-3,-2,-1,0,1,2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false.  For example, $5\\in A$ but $5\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true, while $B⊈A$ is false. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $A⊇B$ is equivalent to $B⊆A,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only\"." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240983 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240541 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240983 1069 "q-105097" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240983 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240983 1071 "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ Find $a+\\frac{1}{2}b.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240983 1072 #uuid "2f51340b-4ecb-59b0-b959-cd7c0eb8679c" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240983 1074 "First, we multiply each component of $b$ by $\\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, we add the vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+\\frac{1}{2}b & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+3 \\\\ -4+3 \\\\ 1+(-1) \\\\ 0+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240984 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240757 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240984 1069 "q-105100" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240984 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240984 1071 "Let $V=C[-1,1],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$ \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=3t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}+1,\\,g(t)=10{t}^{3}\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t)}$ equals $6t.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240984 1072 #uuid "2794de9d-8dc5-594e-8a2b-e907f92bf64b" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240984 1074 "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=6t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}10{t}^{3}\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}10{t}^{3}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $10{t}^{3}$ is an odd function, while ${t}^{2}+1$ is even. Hence, their product $10{t}^{3}({t}^{2}+1)$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-1,1],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}10{t}^{3}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{0}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)=0.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =6t+0 \\\\  & =6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240985 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240546 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240985 1069 "q-105107" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240985 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240985 1071 "Let $r=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $s=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ What is $(\\frac{1}{3}r)\\cdot s?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240985 1072 #uuid "4957802d-fba2-523f-8466-48584c16d24e" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240985 1074 "First, multiplying each component of $r$ by $\\frac{1}{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}r & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the dot product, we add up the products of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{3}r)\\cdot s & =5\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-7)+2\\cdot 6+(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5-21+12-2 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240986 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240496 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240986 1069 "q-105109" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240986 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240986 1071 "Let\n$a=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ x \\\\ 9 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].$\nIf $a\\cdot b=-3$, find the value of $x.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240986 1072 #uuid "77f35aac-e704-5a95-844e-daab6e3d4392" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240986 1074 "Starting from the equation $a\\cdot b=-3,$ we compute the dot product in terms of $x$ and then solve for $x$ in the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =-3 \\\\ x\\cdot 3+2\\cdot x+1\\cdot 9+(-1)\\cdot 7 & =-3 \\\\ 3x+2x+9-7 & =-3 \\\\ 5x & =-5 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $x=-1.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240987 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240503 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240987 1069 "q-105110" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240987 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240987 1071 "Let\n$p=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ y-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $q=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].$\nIf $p\\cdot q=0$, find the value of $y.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240987 1072 #uuid "6e7f4244-4c14-5e9e-9a61-e81225c2df8e" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240987 1074 "Starting from the equation $p\\cdot q=0,$ we compute the dot product in terms of $y$ and then solve for $y$ in the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 9\\cdot 0+(y-3)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-4)+4\\cdot y & =0 \\\\ y-3-12+4y & =0 \\\\ 5y & =15 \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y=3.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240988 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240651 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240988 1069 "q-105113"