894" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240989 1074 "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is an odd function, while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is even. Hence, their product $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) & =\\frac{-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =\\frac{-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240443 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240990 1069 "q-105152" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240990 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240990 1071 "Find $h$, if $3{a}_{1}+h{a}_{2}=b$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240990 1072 #uuid "b99af0d8-b75f-54bf-9697-c5d904e35653" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240990 1074 "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $h$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}+h{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+h[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ h[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ h[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that $h=2.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240991 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240509 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240991 1069 "q-105154" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240991 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186240991 1071 "Find $m$, if $7{a}_{1}+m{a}_{2}=b$ 