-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statement III is false, while statements I and II are true." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241040 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240691 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241040 1069 "q-105313" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241040 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241040 1071 "![](images/da/da3040b284606927a6653bfe18697bff0032dea2647d8d36d857c97472bbc540.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241040 1072 #uuid "f59eef5f-b761-5dcb-b049-d0c57f2862dd" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241040 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/c1/c1491f04d1e3d4692dc362008b13ff3909e7ae6f554a5032a9a93036ee2452b5.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$ The point $B$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and III only'." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241041 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240768 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241041 1069 "q-105314" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241041 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241041 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ if $y={5}^{x}sec⁡x.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241041 1072 #uuid "dd297ed3-dba2-5110-9b3e-0d62167228f9" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241041 1074 "We calculate the derivative using the product rule, as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({5}^{x}sec⁡x) \\\\  & ={5}^{x}ln⁡5sec⁡x+{5}^{x}sec⁡xtan⁡x \\\\  & ={5}^{x}sec⁡x(ln⁡5+tan⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241042 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240389 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241042 1069 "q-105315" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241042 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241042 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241042 1072 #uuid "e39d7a95-6796-5061-a2aa-706f3f881251" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241042 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 9{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}-12{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -5 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+3(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3{x}_{1}+2(0)+5(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are true, while statement I is false." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241043 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241043 1069 "q-105316" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241043 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241043 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ if $y=3sin⁡x+sec⁡x.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241043 1072 #uuid "c5d31a05-3052-5924-9370-b49e037bb225" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241043 1074 "We take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3sin⁡x+sec⁡x) \\\\  & =3cos⁡x+sec⁡xtan⁡x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241044 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240655 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241044 1069 "q-105319" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241044 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241044 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -15 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241044 1072 #uuid "81d16fc8-2819-579f-80e6-748f22f14098" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241044 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 8{x}_{1}-15{x}_{2}+20{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -6{x}_{1}+12{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -15 & 20 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 12 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 12 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+4(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-2{x}_{1}+4(0)-4(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements I and II are true, while statement III is false." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241045 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240755 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241045 1069 "q-105324" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241045 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241045 1071 "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=0$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2\\pi $ | $\\pi $ | $\\pi $ | $0$ |\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})$ at $x=0.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241045 1072 #uuid "b918e5d0-0975-5e7a-bb6f-af0cb342bc44" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241045 1074 "Using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)g(x)-f(x){g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=0,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})|}_{x=0} & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(0)g(0)-f(0){g}^{′}(0)}{(g(0))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\cdot \\pi -2\\pi \\cdot 0}{{\\pi }^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241046 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240336 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241046 1069 "q-105334" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241046 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241046 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>4},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ is a boundary point of $A$\n- The point $Q(2,2)$ is an interior point of $A$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241046 1072 #uuid "2c2c7d13-747e-5ac0-bf88-b6f7f16d61aa" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241046 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie outside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/a1/a1371f9abdeae5083171f5ddfa5d3e237b7e223ba2ba5cf8a822322c34a4676c.png)\n\n \n\nLet's add the points $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ and $Q(2,2)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/02/021ebd260ca399ee47bf8268d02a1a05019d63eef8ab2f5facc56f8576195a80.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ do not satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>4.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ satisfy the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $Q$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is fully contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241047 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240421 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241047 1069 "q-105335" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241047 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241047 1071 "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4-4y-4x & 4-4x+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241047 1072 #uuid "9136d3e6-f2a0-5b9c-a6a2-efe0a42ad6ee" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241047 1074 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4-4y-4x & 4-4x+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4-4y-4x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4-4x+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4-4y-4x=0 \\\\ 4-4x+{y}^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n4-4y-4x=0\\,⟹\\,x=1-y.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-4(1-y)+{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+4y & =0 \\\\ y(y+4) & =0 \\\\ y & =-4,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $x=1-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny=-4\\,⟹\\,x=1-(-4)=5,\\,\\,y=0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,x=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(1,0)$ and $(5,-4)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-4y-4x)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-4x+{y}^{2})=-4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-4y-4x)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-4x+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(1,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,0)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(1,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,0)=det({f}^{″}(1,0))=(-4)\\cdot 0-(-4)^{2}=-16<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(1,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(5,-4),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(5,-4)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(5,-4),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(5,-4)=det({f}^{″}(5,-4))=(-4)\\cdot (-8)-(-4)^{2}=16>0\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(5,-4)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(5,-4)={h}_{11}=-4<0,$ the function $f$ has a local maximum at $(5,-4).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,0)$ and a local maximum at $(5,-4).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241048 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240596 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241048 1069 "q-105357" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241048 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241048 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 9},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(3,0)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(3,0)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(0,2)$ is an interior point of $A$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241048 1072 #uuid "26f835f8-28cf-54e5-acfe-863ecc606ff1" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241048 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or outside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/b9/b9b92085d112ab87592aa8c4d6a56aa4a61ea859d560bac316ce8c97a0af20e5.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(3,0)$ and $Q(0,2)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/27/27806c9bb67b60d52cfbae8e881d307d71a3a5fb9465f36d978035f74d78f4bc.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(3,0)$ satisfy the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(3,0)$ satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 9.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $Q$ is *not* an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only'." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241049 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240743 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241049 1069 "q-105365" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241049 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241049 1071 "The set $B$ and its boundary $∂B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}<12},\\,∂B={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=12}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(3,4)$ lies on the boundary of $B$\n- The point $P(3,4)$ belongs to $B$\n- The point $Q(-3,6)$ is an interior point of $B$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241049 1072 #uuid "f57db6a6-9edb-5f0f-98dd-62f31492deff" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241049 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $B$ consists of all points that lie inside the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=12.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/ad/adee860d39b4d9c4511db2b2f1ad6e7f46342cc474da47303af742be1cc911b3.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(3,4)$ and $Q(-3,6)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/21/2185e7129cd799eb1cbb888789566a406b1594dd5a649b9129bec491a1dce1c7.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(3,4)$ does not satisfy the equation $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=12.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie on the boundary of $B.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(3,4)$ satisfy the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}<12.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $B.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $Q$ contains points that belong to $B$ and points that do not belong to $B.$ The point $Q$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241050 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240424 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241050 1069 "q-105394" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241050 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241050 1071 "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $[-1,2)?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241050 1072 #uuid "7b4259f0-0bad-545a-95ff-791bd00a95c5" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241050 1074 "The correct answer is the following.\n\n![](images/10/105d87736460f2949a0cde4c7fe7cc105e72889312a529970d74080b14e3ffe8.png)\n\n \n\nIndeed, we have that $f(x)$ is continuous on the open interval $(-1,2).$ \n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=-1.$ Hence, it is continuous on the interval $[-1,2).$\n\nNotice that\n\n- $h(x)$ is not right-continuous at $x=-1.$\n\n- $p(x)$ and $g(x)$ are not continuous at some point that lies inside the interval $[-1,2).$\n\n- $q(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$, and so is not continuous on $[-1,2).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241051 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240612 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241051 1069 "q-105396" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241051 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241051 1071 "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $(-3,1]?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241051 1072 #uuid "2c3867ed-69f2-5504-8f0e-9afc7c9c46b2" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241051 1074 "The correct answer is the following.\n\n![](images/44/44740f82eec4457d2772aee7e292f1ab5f908723487a7f195a86a356ea5af810.png)\n\nIndeed, we have that $g(x)$ is continuous on the open interval $(-3,1).$ \n\nAdditionally, the function is left-continuous at $x=1.$ Hence, it is continuous on the interval $(-3,1].$\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x)$ is not left-continuous at $x=1.$\n\n- $h(x)$, $p(x)$, and $q(x)$ are not continuous at some point that lies inside the interval $(-3,1].$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241052 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240359 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241052 1069 "q-105400" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241052 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241052 1071 "Let $V=C[-1,1],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$ \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{2},\\,g(t)=15t+3\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t)}$ equals $15t.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241052 1072 #uuid "c464809f-b653-57ad-9d07-cbfb8f516b25" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241052 1074 "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=15t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(15t+3)\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}15{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{15}{4}{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{15}{4}+1)-(\\frac{15}{4}-1) \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{f}_{2}(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{2}\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{5}{t}^{5}|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}-(-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{2}{(\\frac{2}{5})}\\cdot {t}^{2}=5{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =15t+5{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241053 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240725 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241053 1069 "q-105402" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241053 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241053 1071 "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $[2,\\infty )?$ You may assume that all points of discontinuity for each function are shown." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241053 1072 #uuid "c956371b-5162-5df6-9e5f-db0c70bc212f" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241053 1074 "The correct answer is the following.\n\n![](images/fe/feee0332de88587b13bf58e4f860d76483646f41676c0bb335b4a9d5b32b3218.png)\n\nWe see that $p(x)$ is continuous on the interval $(-2,-\\infty ),$ and since $[2,\\infty )$ lies completely inside $(-2,\\infty ),$ it is also continuous on $[2,\\infty ).$\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x)$ and $q(x)$ are not right-continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $g(x)$ and $h(x)$ are not continuous at $x=3,$ and so are not continuous on $[2,\\infty ).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241054 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240441 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241054 1069 "q-105409" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241054 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241054 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{4{sin}^{2}⁡x}{5{x}^{2}}=$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241054 1072 #uuid "75f19d0b-76eb-589a-abaa-1f1225a49ad8" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241054 1074 "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{4{sin}^{2}⁡x}{5{x}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{4{sin}^{2}⁡x}{5{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {[{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241055 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240705 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241055 1069 "q-105411" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241055 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241055 1071 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of real polynomials of degree at most $2,$ and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1).\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)={t}^{2}+t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}-t,\\,q(t)=7t-3\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t)}$ equals $2{t}^{2}+2t.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241055 1072 #uuid "a51f3bc4-dd7d-5428-ae77-e184158026d7" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241055 1074 "Since the set ${{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)=2{t}^{2}+2t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t)=\\frac{⟨q(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{p}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =q(-1){p}_{2}(-1)+q(0){p}_{2}(0)+q(1){p}_{2}(1) \\\\  & =(7t\\,-\\,3){|}_{-1}\\,\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{-1}+(7t\\,-\\,3){|}_{0}\\,\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{0}+(7t\\,-\\,3){|}_{1}\\,\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-10)\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-20,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={p}_{2}(-1){p}_{2}(-1)+{p}_{2}(0){p}_{2}(0)+{p}_{2}(1){p}_{2}(1) \\\\  & =({t}^{2}-t)^{2}{|}_{-1}+({t}^{2}-t)^{2}{|}_{0}+({t}^{2}-t)^{2}{|}_{1} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) & =\\frac{-20}{4}\\cdot ({t}^{2}-t)=-5{t}^{2}+5t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}+2t+(-5{t}^{2}+5t) \\\\  & =-3{t}^{2}+7t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241056 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240634 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241056 1069 "q-105418" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241056 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241056 1071 "![](images/b8/b875f560c133e528e4d934ae2722d14c116fac4352f6cca5089f8ec726fe3660.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]$\n- $x\\in [-2,4]$\n- $x\\in [4,\\infty )$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241056 1072 #uuid "bef182d0-f2c3-5147-8c82-4dead855f78c" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241056 1074 "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,-2].$ Although the function is continuous on $(-\\infty ,-2),$ it is not left-continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[-2,4].$ It is continuous on $(-2,4),$ and also right-continuous at $x=-2$ and left-continuous at $x=4.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $[4,\\infty ).$ Although the function is continuous on $(4,\\infty ),$ it is not right-continuous at $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241057 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240414 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241057 1069 "q-105419" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241057 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241057 1071 "![](images/c7/c75313fbb97784b27ea0c57068e7221e0f9416ac4857b70723fd7ca949d9aba7.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]$\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)$\n- $x\\in [-3,\\infty )$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241057 1072 #uuid "fe40ba69-5c80-5288-8a8c-e5c1e0d1d354" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241057 1074 "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is continuous on $(-\\infty ,-3].$ It is continuous on $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and also left-continuous at $x=-3.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,0)$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=-3.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $[-3,\\infty ).$ Although the function is continuous on $(-3,\\infty ),$ it is not right-continuous at $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241058 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240609 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241058 1069 "q-105423" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241058 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241058 1071 "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0<y\\le {x}^{3}}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-y<0}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,-{x}^{4}<y<{x}^{4}}$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241058 1072 #uuid "d9f390fe-da40-537a-ae87-c6dfb93153a6" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241058 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(1,1).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some boundary points, for example, the point $(1,0).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\n- The third set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(2,0).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(2,16).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241059 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240653 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241059 1069 "q-105426" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241059 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241059 1071 "${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }=$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241059 1072 #uuid "8574b365-99df-52af-a9c4-7d181fbb97bc" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241059 1074 "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }÷\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }÷\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1\\cdot 1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241060 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240529 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241060 1069 "q-105431" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241060 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241060 1071 "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 6,\\,0<{y}^{2}\\le 3}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<{x}^{2}<9,\\,1<{y}^{2}<16}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2},\\,-2\\le x\\le 2}$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241060 1072 #uuid "2d4a59fe-0871-5826-aa72-1fb9c0a2dad6" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241060 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(2,\\sqrt{3}).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(2,0).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241061 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240504 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241061 1069 "q-105433" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241061 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241061 1071 "${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{4\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }=$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241061 1072 #uuid "0a91918b-4080-5b4a-a50f-84435b2d1d0c" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241061 1074 "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{4\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(4\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(4\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot 4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{({lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )=4{cos}^{2}⁡0=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =1\\cdot 4=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241062 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240605 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241062 1069 "q-105435" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241062 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241062 1071 "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{12-4x}+\\sqrt{5x+8}$ is continuous." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241062 1072 #uuid "ddabafb1-8314-5ad2-b242-235155ac26c6" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241062 1074 "Our function consists of two radical functions, so it is continuous over all points in its domain.\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{12-4x}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-4x\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,4x\\le 12\\,⟹\\,x\\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{5x+8}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+8\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,5x\\ge -8\\,⟹\\,x\\ge -\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe domain of $f(x)$ is given by the intersection of the domain of our radical functions:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,3]\\cap [-\\frac{8}{5},\\infty )=[-\\frac{8}{5},3],\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on this interval." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241063 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240542 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241063 1069 "q-105436" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241063 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241063 1071 "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{4+x}+\\sqrt{3x+18}$ is continuous." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241063 1072 #uuid "ca37f7e8-181f-55c3-97d4-d508fce578da" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241063 1074 "Our function consists of two radical functions, so it is continuous over all points in its domain.\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{4+x}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+x\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,x\\ge -4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{3x+18}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+18\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,3x\\ge -18\\,⟹\\,x\\ge -6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe domain of $f(x)$ is given by the intersection of the domain of our radical functions:\n\n$$\n[-4,\\infty )\\cap [-6,\\infty )=[-4,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on this interval." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241064 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240662 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241064 1069 "q-105437" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241064 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241064 1071 "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }x+y<4,\\,-1<x<4}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }0\\le x<4,\\,2<y\\le 4}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }-1\\le x\\le 3,\\,2<y<4}$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241064 1072 #uuid "9d461bff-0ae1-55fc-a03d-01dfa63c39ec" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241064 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(0,3).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(3,2).$\n\n- The third set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(-1,3).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241065 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240748 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241065 1069 "q-105461" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241065 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241065 1071 "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=3t$ and $g(t)=-3t+3$ with respect to the inner product." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241065 1072 #uuid "a70f1b99-0d4b-5ef2-9e7e-0dc7f6d78417" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241065 1074 "The distance between $f(t)=3t$ and $g(t)=-3t+3$ with respect to the given inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-g(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖3t-(-3t+3)‖ \\\\  & =‖6t-3‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨6t-3,6t-3⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨6t-3,6t-3⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6t-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(36{t}^{2}-36t+9)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[12{t}^{3}-18{t}^{2}+9t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(12-18+9)-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{3}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241066 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240405 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241066 1069 "q-105465" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241066 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241066 1071 "Determine the largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x)=-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{2x+16}$ is continuous." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241066 1072 #uuid "977ff0f6-918f-5e62-ab1b-629afb73b4b4" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241066 1074 "This is a rational function, so it is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{2x+16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{2(x+8)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x+8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded point $x=-8.$\n\nTherefore, the domain consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty ,-8)$ and $(-8,\\infty ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-8)\\cup (-8,\\infty )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on these intervals." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241067 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240711 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241067 1069 "q-105470" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241067 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241067 1071 "Determine the largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-2}{x-2}$ is continuous." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241067 1072 #uuid "6f6c2d7b-b255-5cca-a372-0b6a964a09fa" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241067 1074 "This is a rational function, so it is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-2}{x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-2)}{x-2} \\\\  & =x+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $x\\ne 2.$\n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded point $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the domain consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty ,2)$ and $(2,\\infty ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on this union of intervals." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241068 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240562 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241068 1069 "q-105474" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241068 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241068 1071 "Find the largest interval of continuity for the function $f(x)={log}_{5}⁡(9-x).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241068 1072 #uuid "204fc59a-09be-565a-89b0-1a7d59d1b5dd" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241068 1074 "The logarithmic function ${log}_{2}⁡(9-x)$ is continuous at all points in its domain. However, the logarithm is only defined for positive inputs, so to find its domain, we set the expression inside the logarithm to be greater than zero:\n\n$$\n9-x>0\\,⟹\\,x<9\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(9-x)$ is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,9).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241069 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240740 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241069 1069 "q-105475" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241069 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241069 1071 "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=8t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t)=4t-2$ with respect to the inner product." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241069 1072 #uuid "5d460b9a-6ed3-5d23-955f-f3e1840b55a2" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241069 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}8t\\cdot (4t-2)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4t-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (4t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(32{t}^{2}-16t)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-16t+4)\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (4t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{[\\frac{32}{3}{t}^{3}-8{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1}}{[\\frac{16}{3}{t}^{3}-8{t}^{2}+4t]_{0}^{1}}\\cdot (4t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{32}{3}-8)-0}{(\\frac{16}{3}-4)-0}\\cdot (6t-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{3})}{(\\frac{4}{3})}\\cdot (4t+2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (4t-2) \\\\  & =8t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe distance between $f(t)=8t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t)=4t-2$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖8t-(8t-4)‖ \\\\  & =‖4‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨4,4⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨4,4⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}16\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16t\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =16-0 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{16}=4.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240593 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241070 1069 "q-105478" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241070 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241070 1071 "Find the largest interval of continuity for the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(3-18x).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241070 1072 #uuid "e716612d-e557-566c-8ec1-4dfc4dea027a" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241070 1074 "The logarithmic function $\\operatorname{log}⁡(3-18x)$ is continuous at all points in its domain. However, the logarithm is only defined for positive inputs, so to find its domain, we set the expression inside the logarithm to be greater than zero:\n\n$$\n3-18x>0\\,⟹\\,18x<3\\,⟹\\,x<\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(3-18x)$ is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{6}).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241071 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240756 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241071 1069 "q-105488" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241071 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241071 1071 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $2$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $p(t)=5t$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t)=t-1$ with respect to the inner product." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241071 1072 #uuid "a014caff-6523-5f53-81ab-1541b84035b6" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241071 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{q(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)}{q(-1)q(-1)+q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1)}\\cdot q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(5t){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{t=i}}{{\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(t-1){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{t=i}}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-5)\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 0}{(-2)\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =2(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe distance between $p(t)=5t$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t)=t-1$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖p(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖5t-2(t-1)‖ \\\\  & =‖3t+2‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨3t+2,3t+2⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3t+2,3t+2⟩ & ={\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(3t+2){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (3t+2){|}_{t=i} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 2+5\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =1+4+25 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{30}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241072 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240686 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241072 1069 "q-105492" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241072 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241072 1071 "Let $V={R}_{1}[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=5t$ and $g(t)=7t-8$ with respect to the inner product." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241072 1072 #uuid "bc991422-610d-553b-bc19-40f8543b7b15" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241072 1074 "The distance between $f(t)=5t$ and $g(t)=7t-8$ with respect to the given inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-g(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖5t-(7t-8)‖ \\\\  & =‖-2t+8‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨-2t+8,-2t+8⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨-2t+8,-2t+8⟩ & =(-2t+8){|}_{0}\\cdot (-2t+8){|}_{0}+(-2t+8){|}_{1}\\cdot (-2t+8){|}_{1} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 8+6\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{100}=10.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241073 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240423 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241073 1069 "q-105493" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241073 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241073 1071 "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=0$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nIf $h(x)=\\frac{3f(x)-1}{2g(x)+2},$ determine the value of ${h}^{′}(0).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241073 1072 #uuid "4d869770-47ce-54ee-b130-08e77ddc6f6c" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241073 1074 "The quotient rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{u}{v})=\\frac{v{u}^{′}-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=3f(x)-1$ and $v=2g(x)+2.$ Differentiating each, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the quotient rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{v{u}^{′}-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2g(x)+2)\\cdot 3{f}^{′}(x)-(3f(x)-1)\\cdot 2{g}^{′}(x)}{(2g(x)+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{f}^{′}(x)(2g(x)+2)-2{g}^{′}(x)(3f(x)-1)}{(2g(x)+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=0$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{3{f}^{′}(0)(2g(0)+2)-2{g}^{′}(0)(3f(0)-1)}{(2g(0)+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 0\\cdot (2\\cdot 1+2)-2\\cdot 2\\cdot (3\\cdot 3-1)}{(2\\cdot 1+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\cdot 8}{{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241074 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240732 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241074 1069 "q-105494" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241074 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241074 1071 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $2$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $p(t)=7{t}^{2}-t$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t)=4{t}^{2}-4t$ with respect to the inner product." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241074 1072 #uuid "01b69a97-cb77-5e5f-81f3-d25593886dec" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241074 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{q(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)}{q(-1)q(-1)+q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1)}\\cdot q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(7{t}^{2}-t){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (4{t}^{2}-4t){|}_{t=i}}{{\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(4{t}^{2}-4t){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (4{t}^{2}-4t){|}_{t=i}}\\cdot (4{t}^{2}-4t) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\cdot 8+0\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 0}{8\\cdot 8+0\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 0}\\cdot (4{t}^{2}-4t) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{64}\\cdot (4{t}^{2}-4t) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}-4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe distance between $p(t)=7{t}^{2}-t$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t)=4{t}^{2}-4t$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖p(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖7{t}^{2}-t-(4{t}^{2}-4t)‖ \\\\  & =‖3{t}^{2}+3t‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨3{t}^{2}+3t,3{t}^{2}+3t⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3{t}^{2}+3t,3{t}^{2}+3t⟩ & ={\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(3{t}^{2}+3t){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (3{t}^{2}+3t){|}_{t=i} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =0+0+36 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{36}=6.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241075 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240670 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241075 1069 "q-105495" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241075 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241075 1071 "For the functions $p(x)$ and $q(x),$ you're given the following data:\n\n$$\np(10)=-1,\\,{p}^{′}(10)=-2,\\,q(10)=2,\\,{q}^{′}(10)=1\n$$\n\nIf $z(x)=(2p(x)+q(x))(2-p(x))$, compute ${z}^{′}(10).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241075 1072 #uuid "07342c4b-800a-56e7-9f4a-94fbd8dcbb7b" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241075 1074 "The product rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(uv)=u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2p(x)+q(x)$ and $v=2-p(x).$ Differentiating each, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{p}^{′}(x)+{q}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{p}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}(x) & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(2p(x)+q(x))(-{p}^{′}(x))+(2-p(x))(2{p}^{′}(x)+{q}^{′}(x)) \\\\  & =-{p}^{′}(x)(2p(x)+q(x))+(2{p}^{′}(x)+{q}^{′}(x))(2-p(x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=10,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}(10) & =-{p}^{′}(10)(2p(10)+q(10))+(2{p}^{′}(10)+{q}^{′}(10))(2-p(10)) \\\\  & =-(-2)(2(-1)+2)+(2(-2)+1)(2-(-1)) \\\\  & =2(-2+2)-3(2+1) \\\\  & =0-9 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241076 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240514 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241076 1069 "q-105496" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241076 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241076 1071 "Let $V={R}_{1}[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=8t-3$ and $g(t)=3t+2$ with respect to the inner product." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241076 1072 #uuid "0a9984c7-9d50-53ab-9302-5dd592a189c5" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241076 1074 "The distance between $f(t)=8t-3$ and $g(t)=3t+2$ with respect to the given inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-g(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖8t-3-(3t+2)‖ \\\\  & =‖5t-5‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨5t-5,5t-5⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨5t-5,5t-5⟩ & =(5t-5){|}_{0}\\cdot (5t-5){|}_{0}+(5t-5){|}_{1}\\cdot (5t-5){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot (-5)+0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{25}=5.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241077 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240628 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241077 1069 "q-105497" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241077 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241077 1071 "![](images/08/089281519cd8926eb187c67d465666838b8143b4dd86e081ef3c151613fd27fd.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(4).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241077 1072 #uuid "11dcc5e7-45ba-5891-b7c4-d5a2f0ec6038" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241077 1074 "To compute ${f}^{′}(4),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=4.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ on $[3,5].$ Notice that $x=4$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](images/68/68daf42f2d14b0820bd36db73a8abe7b4d3d449c2b5b865b1d2affd70e069b43.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (3,5)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{-3-0}{5-3}=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(4)=-\\frac{3}{2}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241078 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240548 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241078 1069 "q-105498" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241078 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241078 1071 "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=8t-3$ and $g(t)=2t-1$ with respect to the inner product." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241078 1072 #uuid "78c3ca4e-1ab2-52da-b32e-1ddeb67471e2" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241078 1074 "The distance between $f(t)=8t-3$ and $g(t)=2t-1$ with respect to the given inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-g(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖8t-3-(2t-1)‖ \\\\  & =‖6t-2‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨6t-2,6t-2⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨6t-2,6t-2⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6t-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(36{t}^{2}-24t+4)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[12{t}^{3}-12{t}^{2}+4t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(12-12+4)-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{4}=2.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241079 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240412 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241079 1069 "q-105502" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241079 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241079 1071 "![](images/7b/7b44dcfdfef91ada119ea7346b59f337a82bc8cca1e364fd6ea29c9eead5a2fd.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-3)$ where $h(x)=f(x)g(x).$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241079 1072 #uuid "14a411a4-cb36-5f96-a4f0-a43da2d8c9ab" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241079 1074 "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-3)={f}^{′}(-3)\\cdot g(-3)+f(-3)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-3).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-3)=-2$ and $g(-3)=0.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,2)$ equals $1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-3)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,-2)$ equals $3.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-3)=3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-3) & ={f}^{′}(-3)\\cdot g(-3)+f(-3)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-3) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241080 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240362 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241080 1069 "q-105511" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241080 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241080 1071 "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=7t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t)=t-2$ with respect to the inner product." 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241080 1072 #uuid "c26c2acf-e85e-5f98-8d84-d3505431183c" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241080 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}7t\\cdot (t-2)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(7{t}^{2}-14t)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-4t+4)\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{[\\frac{7}{3}{t}^{3}-7{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1}}{[\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+4t]_{0}^{1}}\\cdot (t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{14}{3})}{(\\frac{7}{3})}\\cdot (t-2) \\\\  & =-2t+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe distance between $f(t)=7t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t)=t-2$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖7t-(-2t+4)‖ \\\\  & =‖9t-4‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨9t-4,9t-4⟩}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨9t-4,9t-4⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9t-4)\\cdot (9t-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9t-4)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(81{t}^{2}-72t+16)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(27{t}^{3}-36{t}^{2}+16)\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{7}.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241081 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240682 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241081 1069 "q-105514" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241081 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241081 1071 "![](images/7f/7fbe399bc506bc36f8986b0f62a2b894195f36584cc0dd1a6873ef38c9116f64.png)\n\nLet $U={♣,♡,△,◻,♠,♢}$ be the universal set and let $Q={◻,♠,♢}.$ What is $\\overset{―}{Q}?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241081 1072 #uuid "b0ff2c39-3bb1-511e-88f3-c400dc92e0d2" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241081 1074 "The complement of $Q,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{Q},$ consists of all the elements in $U$ that are not in $Q.$ To represent $\\overset{―}{Q},$ we will shade in all the area of $U$ that is *outside* of $Q,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/c9/c94ed2827be8a72e798295f16870b4873940f5dffe5b8e17786985a97ccbacb0.png)\n\nThe elements in the shaded area are $♣,♡,$ and $△.$ These are the elements of $U$ that are not in $Q.$ Therefore, the complement of $Q$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{Q}={♣,♡,△}.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241082 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240644 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241082 1069 "q-105526" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241082 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241082 1071 "$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of ${u}_{33}$ in the matrix $U?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241082 1072 #uuid "f49c2300-1b7d-5290-8d52-ca21bfa6709d" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241082 1074 "The matrix $U$ in the LU decomposition is obtained from the original matrix $A$ by row-reducing using Gaussian elementation. In particular, to make zeros below the main diagonal, we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${u}_{33}=-3.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241083 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240738 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241083 1069 "q-105550" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241083 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241083 1071 "$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\boxed{} & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -10\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of the entry ${l}_{31}$ in the matrix $L?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241083 1072 #uuid "fd75d958-f32e-5083-ab73-d127f7dbeb1a" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241083 1074 "We want to make $0$ at the position ${a}_{31}$ using Gaussian elimination. To do this, we take the $3$rd row of $A,$ and we add the $1$st row of $A$ multiplied by $-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe entry ${l}_{31}$ of the matrix $L$ is the factor next to ${R}_{1},$ taken with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{l}_{31}=-(-2)=2.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241084 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240552 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241084 1069 "q-105554" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241084 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241084 1071 "Given that $F=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,i+{x}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡y\\,j,$ what is $\\text{div}\\,F?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241084 1072 #uuid "2ed9b106-fbec-5b47-bde6-fc871e3c0688" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241084 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡y) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241085 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240693 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241085 1069 "q-105573" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241085 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241085 1071 "![](images/30/3088d088689bbd0dfd33abe8240c6f02cceb257937fc1fccb8ba3df151dbfaab.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $\\text{div}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F>0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241085 1072 #uuid "c30bad2f-8979-5b57-acae-23ae1c82d606" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241085 1074 "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $y$-components increase as we move vertically away from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241086 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240363 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241086 1069 "q-105575" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241086 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241086 1071 "![](images/11/11d38951b884f17616a61403d793322d0e2ea28910e806fe8f018251508352a1.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=0$\n- $∇\\cdot F>0$\n- $∇\\cdot F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241086 1072 #uuid "d2172851-a396-51c3-bd4e-4e1278b37143" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241086 1074 "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere, the vector field $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241087 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240753 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241087 1069 "q-105576" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241087 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 1