re\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a half-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/5a/5a028284d29850a9561a589c0e9fbf85e2e10e4e7a23d4ab46a765f492955b4f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]$, we get the half-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the half-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241176 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240582 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241176 1069 "q-106405" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241176 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241176 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241176 1072 #uuid "f8cd9927-85e7-5cc4-8971-2d06dd8aa5a8" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241176 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241177 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240736 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241177 1069 "q-106406" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241177 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241177 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241177 1072 #uuid "6677d4ff-0012-5e92-83bc-c53813937d2b" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241177 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 3y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 9{y}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=1.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241178 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240456 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241178 1069 "q-106407" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241178 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241178 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241178 1072 #uuid "9b47425b-d1be-54fd-a4d5-ddff6578df69" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241178 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2}=1.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241179 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240499 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241179 1069 "q-106408" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241179 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241179 1071 "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241179 1072 #uuid "f6811d7d-6469-50eb-bfd3-5c7960859395" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241179 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2 \\\\ x-2 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2(x-2) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4{(x-2)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3 \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2(y-3) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4{(y-3)}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x-2)}^{2}+4{(y-3)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n4{(x-2)}^{2}+4{(y-3)}^{2}=1.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241180 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240431 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241180 1069 "q-106412" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241180 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241180 1071 "![](images/c4/c42f5aac98d31b7a66a60eebae34b5e83cb62f3ba4801595a81dd9bb57706384.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241180 1072 #uuid "f65595d4-58fb-5ba2-ab22-c9fc4bc25274" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241180 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,-8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,-8),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ -8=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -8 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{1}{2})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-t,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241181 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240547 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241181 1069 "q-106420" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241181 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241181 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation ${y}^{2}=12x?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241181 1072 #uuid "d844955b-0850-5b63-8bf2-a685240a2cc5" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241181 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola ${y}^{2}=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case ${y}^{2}=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =12x \\\\ 4p & =12 \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(3){t}^{2},\\,y=2(3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{2},\\,y=6t.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241182 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240463 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241182 1069 "q-106421" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241182 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241182 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation ${y}^{2}=16x?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241182 1072 #uuid "68bb81dd-055f-5068-9ecd-903a999b9ca6" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241182 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola ${y}^{2}=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case ${y}^{2}=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =16x \\\\ 4p & =16 \\\\ p & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(4){t}^{2},\\,y=2(4)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=4{t}^{2},\\,y=8t.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240449 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241183 1069 "q-106438" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241183 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241183 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241183 1072 #uuid "ff3b94a0-936d-5385-88ce-05fc23ae4390" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241183 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-2x,\\,{t}^{2}=-4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-4y$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2} & =-4y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${x}^{2}=-y.$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240652 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241184 1069 "q-106447" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241184 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241184 1071 "![](images/4a/4ac7f5cd3405e3839b829edc19c440c973521a0263f1aaa0a2f8245697779561.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241184 1072 #uuid "9977732a-3153-5e0c-bb69-50c046885f9a" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241184 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-1,2),$ and passes through the point $(1,4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-1,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+1=2pt,\\,y-2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(1,4),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2pt \\\\ 2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+1=2(\\frac{1}{2})t,\\,y-2=(\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+1=t,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240432 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241185 1069 "q-106452" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241185 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241185 1071 "![](images/0c/0c37bb9e010bdb19de2afc8707b6cf1cef69638c9f4721c407c1593f3a387b87.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241185 1072 #uuid "fc08a2c7-414a-5909-a99f-88078655463f" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241185 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-2,-3),$ and passes through the point $(-12,-13).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-2,-3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+2=2pt,\\,y+3=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-12,-13),$ we can substitute $x=-12$ and $y=-13$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-10=2pt \\\\ -10=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =2pt \\\\ -5 & =pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{5}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =p{(-\\frac{5}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -10 & =p\\cdot \\frac{25}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -10 & =\\frac{25}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+2=2(-\\frac{5}{2})t,\\,y+3=(-\\frac{5}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+2=-5t,\\,y+3=-\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241186 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240728 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241186 1069 "q-106455" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241186 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241186 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(3,3)$ and which passes through the point $(7,5)?$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241186 1072 #uuid "36c7a66b-6181-5e37-8eb4-d8f285049a59" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241186 1074 "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(3,3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-3=2pt,\\,y-3=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(7,5),$ we can substitute $x=7$ and $y=5$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ 2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{2}{p} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-3=2(2)t,\\,y-3=(2){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-3=4t,\\,y-3=2{t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241187 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240618 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241187 1069 "q-106459" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241187 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241187 1071 "![](images/0e/0e7558b9858668e619e3f7750efd76043981e2b833bcda7c7e462f6ecda1aa10.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241187 1072 #uuid "7ada4fca-3977-5aa2-9367-6587a8a4d441" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241187 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-2,3),$ and passes through the point $(6,11).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-2,3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+2=p{t}^{2},\\,y-3=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(6,11),$ we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=11$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}8=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 8=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =2pt \\\\ 4 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{4}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =p{(\\frac{4}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{16}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{16}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{16}{8} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+2=(2){t}^{2},\\,y-3=2(2)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-3=4t.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240583 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241188 1069 "q-106460" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241188 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241188 1071 "![](images/c6/c6496d117c1b769474c353b0bfeef6836d21f080fb108752d55b8bb090dc0243.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241188 1072 #uuid "6e1ad33b-6f45-54cc-bce8-15300f8254f7" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241188 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-5,-3),$ and passes through the point $(7,9).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-5,-3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+5=p{t}^{2},\\,y+3=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(7,9),$ we can substitute $x=7$ and $y=9$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}12=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 12=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =2pt \\\\ 6 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{6}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =p{(\\frac{6}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =p\\cdot \\frac{36}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{36}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{36}{12} \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+5=(3){t}^{2},\\,y+3=2(3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+5=3{t}^{2},\\,y+3=6t.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240385 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241189 1069 "q-106461" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241189 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241189 1071 "![](images/e5/e5c7f025b6f12599a4ecb5fa0cc9e657fd0b67deb1e50231673113f337ca7776.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241189 1072 #uuid "53bdcf57-c309-5f31-a918-fde0b0925ad9" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241189 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(4,4),$ and passes through the point $(8,8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(4,4),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-4=p{t}^{2},\\,y-4=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(8,8),$ we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=8$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 4=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-4=(1){t}^{2},\\,y-4=2(1)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-4={t}^{2},\\,y-4=2t.\n$$" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241190 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240668 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241190 1069 "q-106462" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241190 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241190 1071 "![](images/6c/6cd9c6a97d450761c01a9adb723985acbf4e5dc91e9877d7bd46c95db66d8c30.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241190 1072 #uuid "3121c56e-f3f2-5073-8345-2396c3bdf3b8" 13194139533392 17592186045585 true] [17592186241190 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(3,1),$ and passes through the point $(-5,-7).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(3,1),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-3=p{t}^{2},\\,y-1=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,-7),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=-7$ into 